# Objectives

The primary objective of the **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)** is to establish a stable, authoritative, and scalable identity framework for every Canonical Asset Object within the Coz We Care ecosystem.

CWC-CAR seeks to ensure that every Canonical Asset Object is uniquely identifiable, permanently registered, and consistently traceable throughout its entire lifecycle. By providing a single authoritative registry for object identity, CWC-CAR enables all canonical projects to reference the same asset without ambiguity or duplication.

The objectives of CWC-CAR are to:

- Establish a single authoritative registry for all Canonical Asset Objects.
- Assign one permanent and immutable Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID) to every registered object.
- Eliminate duplicate object identities across the Canonical Asset System.
- Maintain consistent object classification and registration throughout the ecosystem.
- Preserve object identity independently of specifications, implementations, and visual representations.
- Support traceability between Canonical Asset Objects and their related canonical documents.
- Provide the identity foundation for the CWC Canonical Asset Bible (CWC-CAB) and the CWC Canonical Reference Sheet (CWC-CRS).
- Enable long-term scalability while maintaining canonical consistency and interoperability.

By fulfilling these objectives, CWC-CAR serves as the identity infrastructure of the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System, ensuring that every Canonical Asset Object can be managed, referenced, and governed through a single, stable, and authoritative registry.

---

# Project Authority

The **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)** is the sole authoritative registry for the identity of every Canonical Asset Object within the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System.

As the identity authority, CWC-CAR exclusively governs the creation, registration, assignment, maintenance, and retirement of Canonical Object Identifiers (Object IDs). Every Canonical Asset Object shall obtain its identity through CWC-CAR before it may be specified by the CWC Canonical Asset Bible (CWC-CAB) or represented by the CWC Canonical Reference Sheet (CWC-CRS).

No project, document, or registry outside CWC-CAR is permitted to create, modify, duplicate, reassign, or retire Canonical Object Identifiers. Any reference to a Canonical Asset Object within the Canonical Asset System shall use the Object ID assigned by CWC-CAR as the sole authoritative identifier.

The authority of CWC-CAR is limited to object identity and registry governance. It does not define the specifications, implementation, technical behavior, production guidance, or visual representation of Canonical Asset Objects. Those responsibilities remain within their respective canonical projects.

The authority of the three Canonical Asset System projects is therefore clearly separated:

| Project | Primary Authority |
|----------|-------------------|
| **CWC-CAR** | Canonical Asset Object Identity |
| **CWC-CAB** | Canonical Asset Specification |
| **CWC-CRS** | Canonical Asset Reference Representation |

This separation of authority preserves the integrity of the Canonical Asset System by ensuring that identity, specification, and representation remain independent yet fully interoperable throughout the lifecycle of every Canonical Asset Object.

---

# Relationship with CWC-CAB and CWC-CRS

The **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)** operates as one of the three foundational components of the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System, together with the **CWC Canonical Asset Bible (CWC-CAB)** and the **CWC Canonical Reference Sheet (CWC-CRS)**.

Although these three projects work together throughout the canonical asset lifecycle, each has a distinct and independent responsibility. This separation ensures that identity, specification, and reference representation remain clearly defined and independently governed.

Within the Canonical Asset System:

- **CWC-CAR** establishes and owns the identity of every Canonical Asset Object through the assignment and maintenance of Canonical Object Identifiers (Object IDs).
- **CWC-CAB** defines the canonical specification of registered Canonical Asset Objects, including their architecture, standards, governance, and technical definitions.
- **CWC-CRS** provides the canonical reference representation of registered Canonical Asset Objects through standardized visual references and supporting annotations.

The relationship between these projects follows a strict dependency model. Every Canonical Asset Object shall first be registered within CWC-CAR before any canonical specification is created in CWC-CAB or any reference representation is produced in CWC-CRS.

This relationship can be summarized as follows:

```text
Canonical Asset Object
        │
        ▼
CWC-CAR
(Register Object + Assign Object ID)
        │
        ▼
CWC-CAB
(Define Canonical Specification)
        │
        ▼
CWC-CRS
(Create Canonical Reference Representation)
```

CWC-CAB and CWC-CRS depend upon the identities established by CWC-CAR. Neither project may create, modify, duplicate, or retire Canonical Object Identifiers. Likewise, CWC-CAR does not define asset specifications or visual references, as those responsibilities belong exclusively to CWC-CAB and CWC-CRS.

By maintaining this clear separation of responsibilities, the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System preserves a stable, scalable, and interoperable architecture in which every Canonical Asset Object possesses one authoritative identity, one canonical specification, and one canonical reference representation.

---

# Canonical Principles

The **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)** is governed by a set of foundational principles that define how Canonical Asset Objects are identified, registered, and maintained throughout their lifecycle. These principles provide the architectural foundation of the registry and ensure that object identity remains stable, consistent, and independent across the entire Coz We Care Canonical Asset System.

## Single Source of Truth

CWC-CAR is the sole authoritative source for the identity of every Canonical Asset Object. All Canonical Object Identifiers (Object IDs) originate exclusively from this registry.

## One Concept, One Home

Each architectural concept shall have one authoritative home. Object identity belongs exclusively to CWC-CAR, while specifications and reference representations belong to their respective canonical projects.

## Registry Before Specification

A Canonical Asset Object shall be formally registered and assigned an Object ID before any canonical specification is created.

## Registry Before Reference

A Canonical Asset Object shall be formally registered before any canonical reference representation is produced.

## One Object, One Identifier

Every Canonical Asset Object shall possess exactly one permanent Canonical Object Identifier. No object may receive multiple identifiers, and no identifier may represent multiple objects.

## Stable Identifier

Once assigned, a Canonical Object Identifier is permanent and immutable. Changes to specifications, implementations, or representations shall never alter the identity of the registered object.

## Canonical Object Independence

The identity of a Canonical Asset Object exists independently of its specification, implementation, documentation, or visual representation. The registered object remains the same regardless of how it evolves over time.

## Asset Sufficiency

A Canonical Asset Object shall represent a complete and meaningful architectural concept worthy of independent registration. Objects shall not be created solely to satisfy documentation or implementation convenience.

## Registry Integrity

The registry shall maintain a complete, unique, and internally consistent record of every Canonical Asset Object. Duplicate registrations, conflicting identities, and ambiguous object relationships are not permitted.

## Traceable Relationships

Every registered Canonical Asset Object shall support explicit and traceable relationships with other registered objects, enabling consistent navigation across the Canonical Asset System.

## AI-First Knowledge Architecture

The registry shall be structured to maximize machine readability, semantic consistency, and long-term interoperability. Every registration shall support reliable interpretation by both human contributors and AI systems without requiring implicit knowledge or contextual assumptions.

Together, these principles establish the architectural foundation of CWC-CAR and ensure that the registry remains a stable, authoritative, and scalable identity system for every Canonical Asset Object within the Coz We Care ecosystem.

---

# Registry Architecture

The **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)** is organized as a structured identity registry that provides a stable framework for registering, managing, and maintaining every Canonical Asset Object throughout its lifecycle.

The registry architecture is designed to separate object identity from specification and reference representation, allowing each Canonical Asset Object to be managed independently while remaining consistently connected to related canonical projects.

At its core, the registry consists of a collection of **Registry Records**, where each record represents exactly one Canonical Asset Object. Every Registry Record is uniquely identified by a permanent Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID) and contains the minimum information required to establish the object's canonical identity.

Each Registry Record serves as the authoritative source for:

- Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID)
- Object Name
- Object Classification
- Registry Status
- Parent–Child Relationships
- Cross-Object Relationships
- Lifecycle Status
- Registry Metadata

The registry architecture follows a hierarchical yet extensible structure that allows Canonical Asset Objects to be organized according to their logical relationships without affecting their individual identities. Object relationships provide navigational context but never alter or redefine the identity of a registered object.

Conceptually, the registry architecture may be represented as follows:

```text
Canonical Asset Registry
        │
        ├── Registry Record
        │       ├── Object ID
        │       ├── Object Classification
        │       ├── Registry Metadata
        │       ├── Relationships
        │       └── Lifecycle
        │
        ├── Registry Record
        │       └── ...
        │
        └── Registry Record
                └── ...
```

The registry architecture is intentionally minimal. It records only the information necessary to establish and preserve the canonical identity of each object. Specifications, implementation details, governance rules, production guidance, and visual references remain outside the scope of CWC-CAR and are maintained by their respective canonical projects.

This architectural separation ensures that the registry remains stable even as Canonical Asset Objects evolve through new specifications, updated implementations, or revised reference representations. As a result, CWC-CAR provides a durable identity infrastructure capable of supporting long-term scalability, interoperability, and canonical consistency across the entire Coz We Care ecosystem.

---

# Registry Structure

The **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)** is composed of individual **Registry Records**, each representing a single Canonical Asset Object. Every Registry Record serves as the authoritative identity record for its corresponding object and contains only the information required to establish, identify, classify, and trace that object throughout its lifecycle.

Each Registry Record follows a standardized structure to ensure consistency, interoperability, and long-term maintainability across the Canonical Asset System.

A Registry Record consists of the following core components:

| Component | Description |
|------------|-------------|
| **Object ID** | The permanent and unique Canonical Object Identifier assigned by CWC-CAR. |
| **Object Name** | The official canonical name of the registered object. |
| **Object Type** | The classification category of the Canonical Asset Object. |
| **Object Description** | A concise description defining the conceptual identity of the object. |
| **Registry Status** | The current registration status of the object. |
| **Lifecycle State** | The current lifecycle stage of the object within the registry. |
| **Parent Object** | The immediate parent object, if applicable. |
| **Child Objects** | Any directly related subordinate objects. |
| **Related Objects** | Other Canonical Asset Objects with defined relationships. |
| **Canonical References** | References to corresponding entries in CWC-CAB and CWC-CRS. |
| **Registry Metadata** | Administrative metadata supporting registry management and traceability. |

Each Registry Record represents exactly one Canonical Asset Object. No Registry Record may represent multiple objects, and no Canonical Asset Object may possess more than one Registry Record.

The registry structure is intentionally independent of implementation details. It records only the canonical identity of an object and the relationships necessary to support consistent navigation and governance throughout the Canonical Asset System.

By standardizing the structure of every Registry Record, CWC-CAR ensures that all Canonical Asset Objects are registered in a uniform, predictable, and machine-readable manner while preserving the stability and integrity of the overall registry.

---

# Canonical Asset Object

A **Canonical Asset Object** is the fundamental unit of identity within the **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)**. It represents a uniquely recognized architectural concept that has been formally registered within the Canonical Asset System and assigned a permanent **Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID)**.

A Canonical Asset Object exists independently of its specification, implementation, documentation, or visual representation. Its identity remains constant throughout its entire lifecycle, regardless of how its associated specifications or reference materials evolve over time.

The purpose of a Canonical Asset Object is to provide a stable and unambiguous identity that can be consistently referenced across all canonical projects. Once registered, the object becomes the authoritative subject upon which canonical specifications are defined within the **CWC Canonical Asset Bible (CWC-CAB)** and canonical reference representations are produced within the **CWC Canonical Reference Sheet (CWC-CRS)**.

Every Canonical Asset Object shall satisfy the following characteristics:

- It represents exactly one distinct architectural concept.
- It possesses exactly one permanent Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID).
- It is registered exactly once within CWC-CAR.
- Its identity is independent of specifications, implementations, and visual references.
- It may participate in one or more canonical relationships with other registered objects.
- It remains traceable throughout its entire lifecycle.

A Canonical Asset Object is not a document, file, diagram, template, implementation artifact, or visual asset. Rather, it is the conceptual identity that those resources describe, implement, or represent.

Conceptually, the relationship can be illustrated as follows:

```text
Canonical Asset Object
        │
        ├── Identity (CWC-CAR)
        │
        ├── Specification (CWC-CAB)
        │
        └── Reference Representation (CWC-CRS)
```

By separating object identity from specification and representation, the Canonical Asset System ensures that every Canonical Asset Object maintains a single, stable, and authoritative identity while allowing its associated documentation and reference materials to evolve independently.

---

# Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID)

A **Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID)** is the permanent and unique identifier assigned to every Canonical Asset Object registered within the **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)**.

The Object ID serves as the authoritative identity of a Canonical Asset Object throughout its entire lifecycle. It provides a stable reference that remains unchanged regardless of modifications to the object's specification, implementation, documentation, or reference representation.

Every Canonical Asset Object shall receive exactly one Object ID at the time of registration. Once assigned, the Object ID becomes immutable and shall never be reused, reassigned, or transferred to another object.

The primary purpose of the Object ID is to ensure that every Canonical Asset Object can be uniquely identified and consistently referenced across all components of the Canonical Asset System. As the common identity shared by CWC-CAR, CWC-CAB, and CWC-CRS, the Object ID enables interoperability, traceability, and long-term canonical consistency.

Each Canonical Object Identifier shall satisfy the following principles:

- It uniquely identifies one and only one Canonical Asset Object.
- It is assigned exclusively by CWC-CAR.
- It remains permanent throughout the object's lifecycle.
- It shall never be modified after assignment.
- It shall never be reused for another object.
- It shall be referenced consistently by all canonical projects.

The Object ID functions solely as an identity mechanism. It carries no semantic meaning regarding the object's specification, implementation, ownership, lifecycle state, or visual representation. Those characteristics are maintained independently within their respective canonical records.

Conceptually, the role of the Object ID can be illustrated as follows:

```text
Canonical Asset Object
          │
          ▼
    Object ID (Permanent Identity)
          │
          ├── CWC-CAR
          │     Registry Record
          │
          ├── CWC-CAB
          │     Canonical Specification
          │
          └── CWC-CRS
                Reference Representation
```

By establishing a permanent and immutable identity for every Canonical Asset Object, the Canonical Object Identifier provides the foundation upon which the entire Coz We Care Canonical Asset System maintains consistency, interoperability, and traceability across all canonical documentation.

---

# Object Registration

Object Registration is the formal process through which a **Canonical Asset Object** is recognized and recorded within the **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)**. Registration establishes the official existence of a Canonical Asset Object within the Canonical Asset System and assigns it a permanent **Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID)**.

Registration is the first mandatory step in the canonical asset lifecycle. No Canonical Asset Object may proceed to canonical specification or reference representation until it has been successfully registered in CWC-CAR.

The purpose of Object Registration is to:

- Establish the official identity of a Canonical Asset Object.
- Assign a unique and permanent Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID).
- Prevent duplicate object identities.
- Ensure consistent classification and traceability.
- Provide the identity foundation for downstream canonical projects.

Each Canonical Asset Object shall be registered exactly once. Once registration is completed, the Registry Record becomes the authoritative identity record for that object throughout its lifecycle.

Object Registration consists of the following conceptual stages:

```text
Candidate Object
        │
        ▼
Identity Validation
        │
        ▼
Object Registration
        │
        ▼
Object ID Assignment
        │
        ▼
Registry Record Creation
        │
        ▼
Registered Canonical Asset Object
```

Successful registration confirms only the existence and identity of the Canonical Asset Object. It does not imply that the object has been fully specified, implemented, approved, or visually represented. Those activities occur independently within the CWC Canonical Asset Bible (CWC-CAB) and the CWC Canonical Reference Sheet (CWC-CRS).

By requiring every Canonical Asset Object to pass through a standardized registration process, CWC-CAR ensures that the Canonical Asset System maintains a complete, consistent, and authoritative registry in which every object possesses a single, permanent, and traceable identity.

---

# Registry Record

A **Registry Record** is the authoritative registration entry for a single **Canonical Asset Object** within the **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)**. It serves as the permanent identity record that documents the existence, identity, classification, and registry metadata of a registered object.

Each Registry Record represents exactly one Canonical Asset Object. Likewise, every Canonical Asset Object shall possess exactly one Registry Record throughout its lifecycle. This one-to-one relationship ensures that object identity remains unique, traceable, and free from duplication.

The Registry Record is the authoritative source for the canonical identity of an object. It establishes the object's official registration within the Canonical Asset System while remaining independent of its specification, implementation, governance, or visual representation.

A Registry Record typically contains the following information:

- Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID)
- Official Object Name
- Object Classification
- Object Description
- Registry Status
- Lifecycle State
- Parent–Child Relationships
- Related Objects
- Canonical References
- Registry Metadata

The Registry Record intentionally contains only identity-related information. It does not include technical specifications, implementation guidance, design standards, production instructions, or reference illustrations. Those responsibilities belong to their respective canonical projects.

Conceptually, the relationship between a Canonical Asset Object and its Registry Record can be illustrated as follows:

```text
Canonical Asset Object
          │
          ▼
     Registry Record
          │
          ├── Object ID
          ├── Object Name
          ├── Classification
          ├── Relationships
          ├── Lifecycle
          └── Metadata
```

A Registry Record may evolve as registry metadata, classifications, relationships, or lifecycle information are updated. However, the identity of the registered object, as established by its Canonical Object Identifier, shall remain permanent and immutable.

By maintaining one standardized Registry Record for every Canonical Asset Object, CWC-CAR ensures a complete, consistent, and authoritative registry that supports long-term traceability, interoperability, and governance across the entire Coz We Care Canonical Asset System.

---

# Object Classification

**Object Classification** is the process of assigning a Canonical Asset Object to an appropriate classification category within the **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)**. Classification provides a consistent organizational structure that enables Canonical Asset Objects to be discovered, managed, and related without affecting their canonical identity.

The purpose of Object Classification is to group Canonical Asset Objects according to their architectural role within the Canonical Asset System. Classification supports registry organization, navigation, interoperability, and long-term scalability while remaining independent of an object's specification, implementation, or visual representation.

Every Canonical Asset Object shall belong to one primary classification category. This classification reflects the object's conceptual role rather than its implementation or documentation.

Object Classification serves to:

- Organize Canonical Asset Objects within the registry.
- Support consistent registry navigation.
- Facilitate object discovery and traceability.
- Enable meaningful relationships between registered objects.
- Improve long-term maintainability of the Canonical Asset System.

Classification does not determine the authority, lifecycle state, ownership, or behavior of a Canonical Asset Object. It functions solely as a structural mechanism for organizing registered objects.

Conceptually, Object Classification may be represented as follows:

```text
Canonical Asset Object
          │
          ▼
 Object Classification
          │
          ├── Domain
          ├── Category
          ├── Type
          └── Subtype
```

The specific classification taxonomy adopted by CWC-CAR is defined independently from the registration process and may evolve as the Canonical Asset System expands. However, any changes to the classification framework shall not alter the identity of existing Canonical Asset Objects or their assigned Canonical Object Identifiers.

By maintaining a consistent and extensible classification structure, CWC-CAR ensures that every Canonical Asset Object can be systematically organized while preserving the stability, uniqueness, and permanence of its canonical identity.

---

# Object Relationships

**Object Relationships** define the formal associations between **Canonical Asset Objects** registered within the **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)**. These relationships establish how registered objects are connected while preserving the independence and identity of each individual object.

The purpose of Object Relationships is to provide a structured representation of the architectural connections that exist across the Canonical Asset System. By recording these relationships, CWC-CAR enables consistent navigation, dependency tracing, and knowledge integration without altering the identity of any registered object.

Every relationship recorded within CWC-CAR exists between two or more registered Canonical Asset Objects. Relationships describe how objects are associated but do not imply ownership, implementation, or specification unless explicitly defined by their respective canonical projects.

Object Relationships support the following objectives:

- Establish traceable connections between registered objects.
- Enable structured navigation throughout the registry.
- Support dependency analysis across canonical assets.
- Improve interoperability between canonical projects.
- Preserve architectural consistency within the Canonical Asset System.

Relationships are descriptive rather than defining. A relationship provides context about how objects are connected but never changes the identity of a Canonical Asset Object or its assigned Canonical Object Identifier.

Conceptually, Object Relationships may be represented as follows:

```text
Canonical Asset Object A
           │
           │
     Relationship
           │
           ▼
Canonical Asset Object B
```

A Canonical Asset Object may participate in multiple relationships simultaneously. These relationships may represent hierarchical structures, dependencies, associations, compositions, references, or other architectural connections as defined by the registry model.

Changes to Object Relationships shall not modify the identity of any registered object. The Canonical Object Identifier assigned by CWC-CAR remains permanent regardless of how relationships evolve throughout the lifecycle of the Canonical Asset System.

By maintaining explicit and traceable Object Relationships, CWC-CAR strengthens the structural integrity of the registry and enables a coherent, interconnected, and scalable knowledge architecture across the entire Coz We Care ecosystem.

---

# Registry Lifecycle

The **Registry Lifecycle** defines the progression of a **Canonical Asset Object** within the **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)** from its initial recognition through its eventual retirement. The lifecycle provides a consistent framework for managing the registration status of Canonical Asset Objects while preserving the permanence of their canonical identities.

The purpose of the Registry Lifecycle is to ensure that every Canonical Asset Object is managed in a predictable, traceable, and controlled manner throughout its existence within the Canonical Asset System.

A Canonical Asset Object progresses through a series of lifecycle states that reflect its registration status rather than its specification, implementation, or operational use.

The conceptual lifecycle is illustrated below:

```text
Candidate
    │
    ▼
Registered
    │
    ▼
Active
    │
    ├──────────────┐
    ▼              │
Deprecated         │
    │              │
    ▼              │
Archived ◄─────────┘
```

Each lifecycle state represents a distinct stage in the registry:

| Lifecycle State | Description |
|-----------------|-------------|
| **Candidate** | The object has been identified but has not yet been formally registered. |
| **Registered** | The object has been successfully registered and assigned a permanent Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID). |
| **Active** | The object is an active member of the Canonical Asset System and may be referenced by other canonical projects. |
| **Deprecated** | The object is retained for historical and compatibility purposes but is no longer recommended for new canonical use. |
| **Archived** | The object is permanently preserved within the registry as a historical record and is no longer active. |

Transition between lifecycle states shall never alter the identity of a Canonical Asset Object. Once assigned, the Canonical Object Identifier remains permanent regardless of whether the object becomes active, deprecated, or archived.

The Registry Lifecycle governs only the registration status of Canonical Asset Objects. It does not determine the lifecycle of specifications, implementations, documentation, production assets, or visual references, which are managed independently within their respective canonical projects.

By maintaining a standardized Registry Lifecycle, CWC-CAR ensures that every Canonical Asset Object remains consistently traceable while preserving the long-term stability, integrity, and historical continuity of the Canonical Asset System.

---

# Registry Governance

**Registry Governance** establishes the principles, responsibilities, and authority by which the **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)** is maintained. It ensures that every Canonical Asset Object is registered, managed, and preserved in a consistent, controlled, and authoritative manner throughout its lifecycle.

The purpose of Registry Governance is to protect the integrity of the registry by ensuring that object identity remains stable, unique, traceable, and independent of changes occurring within other canonical projects.

As the authoritative registry for Canonical Asset Object identity, CWC-CAR governs:

- Object registration
- Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID) assignment
- Registry Record maintenance
- Object classification
- Object relationships
- Registry lifecycle management
- Registry integrity

Registry Governance does not define the specifications, implementation rules, production standards, or visual representations of Canonical Asset Objects. Those responsibilities remain exclusively within the CWC Canonical Asset Bible (CWC-CAB) and the CWC Canonical Reference Sheet (CWC-CRS).

The governance authority of CWC-CAR is founded upon the following principles:

- Only CWC-CAR may create Canonical Object Identifiers.
- Every Canonical Asset Object shall be registered exactly once.
- Every Registry Record shall represent exactly one Canonical Asset Object.
- Canonical Object Identifiers shall remain permanent and immutable.
- Registry modifications shall preserve the identity of registered objects.
- Registry decisions shall prioritize consistency, traceability, and long-term interoperability.

Conceptually, Registry Governance operates as follows:

```text
Registry Governance
        │
        ├── Registration Authority
        ├── Identity Management
        ├── Classification Management
        ├── Relationship Management
        ├── Lifecycle Management
        └── Registry Integrity
```

Registry Governance applies exclusively to the management of the registry itself. While Canonical Asset Objects may evolve through revised specifications or updated reference representations, their canonical identities remain under the exclusive authority of CWC-CAR.

By establishing clear governance boundaries and responsibilities, CWC-CAR ensures that the Canonical Asset Registry remains a stable, authoritative, and trustworthy identity foundation for the entire Coz We Care Canonical Asset System.

---

# Registry Integrity

**Registry Integrity** is the foundational principle that ensures the **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)** remains accurate, consistent, complete, and authoritative throughout the lifecycle of every Canonical Asset Object.

The purpose of Registry Integrity is to preserve the reliability of the registry by ensuring that every Canonical Asset Object maintains a single, permanent, and unambiguous identity. Registry Integrity protects the Canonical Asset System from duplication, inconsistency, conflicting identities, and structural ambiguity.

Registry Integrity is maintained through the following core principles:

- Every Canonical Asset Object shall possess exactly one Registry Record.
- Every Registry Record shall represent exactly one Canonical Asset Object.
- Every Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID) shall be unique, permanent, and immutable.
- No Registry Record shall duplicate the identity of another registered object.
- Registry relationships shall remain internally consistent and traceable.
- Registry modifications shall preserve the canonical identity of every registered object.

Registry Integrity applies to the structure and identity of the registry rather than to the specifications or visual representations maintained by other canonical projects.

Conceptually, Registry Integrity may be represented as follows:

```text
Registry Integrity
        │
        ├── Identity Uniqueness
        ├── Registration Consistency
        ├── Relationship Consistency
        ├── Structural Completeness
        ├── Traceability
        └── Long-Term Stability
```

Maintaining Registry Integrity requires that every registration, update, relationship, classification, and lifecycle transition be performed without compromising the permanent identity of any Canonical Asset Object. While registry metadata may evolve over time, the Canonical Object Identifier assigned to a registered object shall never change.

Registry Integrity also supports interoperability across the Canonical Asset System by ensuring that CWC-CAB and CWC-CRS always reference the same authoritative Canonical Asset Object identities established by CWC-CAR.

By preserving uniqueness, consistency, completeness, and traceability, Registry Integrity ensures that CWC-CAR remains a trusted, stable, and authoritative identity registry capable of supporting the long-term evolution of the Coz We Care knowledge architecture.

---

# Registry Maintenance

**Registry Maintenance** is the continuous process of preserving the accuracy, consistency, and reliability of the **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)** throughout the lifecycle of every Canonical Asset Object.

The purpose of Registry Maintenance is to ensure that the registry remains an authoritative and up-to-date record of Canonical Asset Object identities while preserving the permanence of Canonical Object Identifiers (Object IDs). Maintenance activities support the ongoing evolution of the Canonical Asset System without compromising registry integrity.

Registry Maintenance applies exclusively to registry information and identity records. It does not include the maintenance of canonical specifications, implementation guidance, or reference representations, which are managed independently by CWC-CAB and CWC-CRS.

Registry Maintenance includes activities such as:

- Registering new Canonical Asset Objects.
- Updating Registry Records when registry metadata changes.
- Managing object classifications.
- Maintaining object relationships.
- Recording lifecycle state transitions.
- Preserving historical registry information.
- Correcting registry metadata when necessary.
- Monitoring registry consistency and integrity.

Registry Maintenance shall always preserve the following invariants:

- A Canonical Object Identifier shall never change after assignment.
- A Registry Record shall never represent more than one Canonical Asset Object.
- A registered object shall never receive a second Object ID.
- Historical registry information shall remain traceable.
- Registry updates shall not invalidate existing canonical references.

Conceptually, Registry Maintenance may be represented as follows:

```text
Registry Maintenance
        │
        ├── Registration
        ├── Metadata Updates
        ├── Classification Updates
        ├── Relationship Updates
        ├── Lifecycle Updates
        ├── Integrity Verification
        └── Historical Preservation
```

Registry Maintenance is an ongoing governance function that supports the long-term sustainability of the Canonical Asset Registry. While registered objects may evolve through changes to their associated specifications or reference representations, their canonical identities remain stable and continuously protected by CWC-CAR.

Through disciplined Registry Maintenance, CWC-CAR ensures that the Canonical Asset Registry remains accurate, trustworthy, scalable, and capable of supporting the continued growth of the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System.

---

# Conclusion

The **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)** establishes the authoritative identity foundation of the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System. By providing a single, permanent, and consistent registry for every Canonical Asset Object, CWC-CAR ensures that canonical identity remains independent of specifications, implementations, and visual representations.

Throughout this document, CWC-CAR has defined the principles, architecture, structure, governance, and lifecycle required to maintain a stable and scalable registry of Canonical Asset Objects. Together, these components form an integrated identity infrastructure that enables every registered object to be uniquely identified, consistently referenced, and reliably governed across the entire ecosystem.

As one of the three foundational projects within the Canonical Asset System, CWC-CAR works in close coordination with the **CWC Canonical Asset Bible (CWC-CAB)** and the **CWC Canonical Reference Sheet (CWC-CRS)**. While each project maintains a distinct responsibility, they collectively support a unified canonical architecture in which identity, specification, and reference representation remain clearly separated yet fully interoperable.

The long-term value of CWC-CAR lies not merely in maintaining a registry, but in safeguarding the continuity of canonical identity as the Coz We Care ecosystem grows and evolves. By preserving stable Object Identifiers, traceable relationships, and consistent registration practices, the registry provides a durable foundation upon which all future canonical assets can be developed with confidence and architectural consistency.

As the authoritative registry of Canonical Asset Objects, CWC-CAR serves as the permanent identity layer of the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System, ensuring that every canonical asset begins with one identity, remains one identity, and is governed through one authoritative registry.

---

# Appendix A — Registry Schema

The **Registry Schema** defines the canonical information model for every **Registry Record** maintained within the **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)**.

Its purpose is to establish a standardized structure that ensures every Canonical Asset Object is registered in a consistent, predictable, and machine-readable manner. By adopting a common schema, CWC-CAR maintains interoperability, traceability, and long-term stability across the entire Canonical Asset System.

The Registry Schema specifies **what information constitutes a Registry Record**, not how that information is implemented or displayed. It provides the structural blueprint for registry records while remaining independent of canonical specifications and reference representations.

Every Registry Record maintained within CWC-CAR shall conform to this Registry Schema.

The Registry Schema is organized into the following logical sections:

| Section | Purpose |
|----------|---------|
| **Object Identity** | Defines the permanent identity of the Canonical Asset Object. |
| **Object Classification** | Describes how the object is categorized within the registry. |
| **Object Relationships** | Records the formal relationships between registered objects. |
| **Registry Lifecycle** | Identifies the current lifecycle state of the registered object. |
| **Canonical References** | Links the object to its corresponding canonical specification and reference representation. |
| **Registry Metadata** | Records administrative information required for registry management and traceability. |

Each section contributes a distinct aspect of the Registry Record while preserving the principle that object identity remains independent of specifications, implementations, governance, and visual representations.

The Registry Schema serves as the canonical template for all Registry Records. Although the registry may evolve to accommodate future architectural needs, the fundamental structure defined by this schema provides the stable foundation upon which every Canonical Asset Object is registered and maintained.

The following sections define each component of the Registry Schema in detail.

---

## A.1 Object Identity

**Object Identity** is the core component of the Registry Schema. It establishes the permanent and authoritative identity of a **Canonical Asset Object** within the **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)**.

The purpose of Object Identity is to ensure that every Canonical Asset Object can be uniquely identified throughout its entire lifecycle, regardless of changes to its specification, implementation, governance, or reference representation.

Object Identity represents the conceptual identity of the registered object rather than any particular document, implementation, or visual artifact. Once established, the identity of a Canonical Asset Object remains constant and shall never be replaced by another identity.

Every Registry Record shall contain exactly one Object Identity.

The Object Identity section consists of the following core elements:

| Element | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| **Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID)** | The permanent and unique identifier assigned by CWC-CAR. |
| **Object Name** | The official canonical name of the registered object. |
| **Object Type** | The primary classification describing the nature of the object. |
| **Object Summary** | A concise description defining the conceptual identity of the object. |
| **Identity Status** | Indicates whether the object's identity is active, deprecated, or archived within the registry. |

The Object Identity section establishes only the canonical identity of the registered object. It does not describe the object's specification, implementation details, production guidance, technical behavior, or visual appearance.

Conceptually, Object Identity may be represented as follows:

```text
Object Identity
       │
       ├── Object ID
       ├── Object Name
       ├── Object Type
       ├── Object Summary
       └── Identity Status
```

Object Identity serves as the permanent anchor for every Registry Record. All subsequent registry information—including classifications, relationships, lifecycle states, and canonical references—is associated with this identity while remaining independent of it.

By defining a single, immutable identity for every Canonical Asset Object, the Object Identity section provides the foundation upon which the entire Canonical Asset Registry is built.

---

## A.2 Object Classification

The **Object Classification** section of the Registry Schema defines the canonical classification assigned to a registered Canonical Asset Object. Classification organizes objects according to their architectural role within the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System while preserving the independence of their canonical identities.

The purpose of Object Classification is to provide a consistent and extensible taxonomy that enables Canonical Asset Objects to be grouped, discovered, navigated, and managed throughout the registry. Classification supports registry organization but does not influence the identity, authority, or lifecycle of a registered object.

Every Registry Record shall contain exactly one primary classification. Additional classifications may be introduced where required by the registry model, provided that they do not alter the object's canonical identity.

The Object Classification section consists of the following core elements:

| Element | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| **Domain** | The highest-level organizational domain to which the object belongs. |
| **Category** | The primary functional grouping of the object. |
| **Type** | The specific architectural type of the object. |
| **Subtype** | An optional specialization of the object type. |
| **Classification Notes** | Optional registry notes supporting classification decisions. |

Conceptually, the classification hierarchy is represented as follows:

```text
Domain
   │
   ▼
Category
   │
   ▼
Type
   │
   ▼
Subtype
```

Object Classification exists solely to organize Canonical Asset Objects within the registry. It shall never replace, modify, or redefine the canonical identity established by the Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID).

Changes to an object's classification shall not affect its identity, Registry Record, or Canonical Object Identifier. The registry may evolve to support additional classification levels or categories while preserving backward compatibility with existing Registry Records.

By maintaining a standardized classification structure, CWC-CAR ensures that every Canonical Asset Object can be systematically organized, consistently managed, and efficiently discovered while preserving the stability, scalability, and interoperability of the Canonical Asset System.

---

## A.3 Object Relationships

The **Object Relationships** section of the Registry Schema defines the formal associations between registered Canonical Asset Objects within the **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)**. These relationships provide the structural context necessary to understand how objects are connected while preserving the independence of each object's canonical identity.

The purpose of Object Relationships is to establish explicit, traceable, and consistent connections between Registry Records. These relationships support registry navigation, architectural analysis, dependency tracking, and knowledge integration without affecting the identity or authority of any registered object.

Every relationship recorded within CWC-CAR shall reference only registered Canonical Asset Objects identified by valid Canonical Object Identifiers (Object IDs).

The Object Relationships section consists of the following core elements:

| Element | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| **Relationship Type** | The canonical type describing the association between objects. |
| **Source Object ID** | The Object ID of the originating Canonical Asset Object. |
| **Target Object ID** | The Object ID of the related Canonical Asset Object. |
| **Relationship Direction** | Indicates the direction of the relationship, where applicable. |
| **Relationship Description** | Optional explanation describing the purpose of the relationship. |

Conceptually, object relationships are represented as follows:

```text
Registry Record
      │
      ▼
Relationship
      │
      ▼
Registry Record
```

A Canonical Asset Object may participate in multiple relationships simultaneously. These relationships provide architectural context but shall never alter, replace, or redefine the canonical identity established by the assigned Canonical Object Identifier.

The complete taxonomy of supported relationship types is defined separately within the Registry Relationship Taxonomy to ensure consistency across the Canonical Asset System.

By maintaining explicit and standardized Object Relationships, CWC-CAR enables a coherent, interconnected, and scalable registry in which Canonical Asset Objects remain independently identifiable while participating in a well-defined architectural network.

---

## A.4 Registry Lifecycle

The **Registry Lifecycle** section of the Registry Schema defines the current registration state of a Canonical Asset Object within the **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)**. It records the progression of a Registry Record throughout its existence while preserving the permanence of the object's canonical identity.

The purpose of the Registry Lifecycle is to provide a standardized mechanism for tracking the registration status of Canonical Asset Objects. Lifecycle information supports governance, registry management, and historical traceability without affecting the Canonical Object Identifier or the conceptual identity of the registered object.

Every Registry Record shall contain exactly one current lifecycle state.

The Registry Lifecycle section consists of the following core elements:

| Element | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| **Lifecycle State** | The current registration state of the Canonical Asset Object. |
| **Effective Date** | The date on which the current lifecycle state became effective. |
| **Previous State** | The immediately preceding lifecycle state, where applicable. |
| **Transition Reason** | The rationale for the lifecycle transition. |
| **Lifecycle Notes** | Optional administrative notes supporting lifecycle management. |

Conceptually, the Registry Lifecycle is represented as follows:

```text
Candidate
    │
    ▼
Registered
    │
    ▼
Active
    │
    ├──────────────┐
    ▼              │
Deprecated         │
    │              │
    ▼              │
Archived ◄─────────┘
```

Lifecycle transitions modify only the registration status of a Registry Record. They shall never alter the Canonical Object Identifier, the canonical identity of the registered object, or the historical continuity of the Registry Record.

The complete definition of lifecycle states and transition policies is maintained separately within the Registry Lifecycle framework to ensure consistent governance across the Canonical Asset System.

By maintaining a standardized Registry Lifecycle, CWC-CAR provides a reliable mechanism for managing the evolution of Registry Records while preserving the stability, traceability, and long-term integrity of every Canonical Asset Object.

---

## A.5 Canonical References

The **Canonical References** section of the Registry Schema defines the formal references that connect a Registry Record to its corresponding canonical documents within the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System.

The purpose of Canonical References is to establish traceable links between the registered Canonical Asset Object and the canonical artifacts that define or represent it. These references enable interoperability across the Canonical Asset System while preserving the independence of each project's responsibility.

Canonical References do not create identity. They simply reference the identity that has already been established by CWC-CAR through the assigned Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID).

Every Registry Record may contain one or more Canonical References.

The Canonical References section consists of the following core elements:

| Element | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| **Object ID** | The Canonical Object Identifier serving as the reference anchor. |
| **Canonical Asset Bible Reference** | Reference to the corresponding specification maintained by CWC-CAB. |
| **Canonical Reference Sheet Reference** | Reference to the corresponding visual representation maintained by CWC-CRS. |
| **Related Registry References** | References to other associated Registry Records within CWC-CAR. |
| **External References** | Optional references to approved external canonical resources, where applicable. |

Conceptually, Canonical References are represented as follows:

```text
Registry Record
      │
      ├── CWC-CAB
      │      Specification
      │
      ├── CWC-CRS
      │      Reference Representation
      │
      └── Related Registry Records
```

Canonical References are informational links only. They shall never replace, redefine, or duplicate the Canonical Object Identifier assigned by CWC-CAR. The identity of every Canonical Asset Object remains exclusively under the authority of the Registry.

By maintaining standardized Canonical References, CWC-CAR enables seamless navigation between identity, specification, and reference representation while preserving the clear separation of responsibilities defined by the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System.

---

## A.6 Registry Metadata

The **Registry Metadata** section of the Registry Schema records the administrative information associated with a Registry Record. Unlike Object Identity, Object Classification, Object Relationships, and Registry Lifecycle, Registry Metadata exists solely to support the management, traceability, and maintenance of the registry itself.

The purpose of Registry Metadata is to provide contextual information that enables Registry Records to be administered consistently throughout their lifecycle while preserving the independence and permanence of the registered Canonical Asset Object.

Registry Metadata does not define or modify the identity of a Canonical Asset Object. Likewise, changes to metadata shall never affect the assigned Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID), object classification, or canonical relationships.

Every Registry Record shall contain one Registry Metadata section.

The Registry Metadata section consists of the following core elements:

| Element | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| **Record Version** | The current version of the Registry Record. |
| **Record Status** | The administrative status of the Registry Record. |
| **Registration Date** | The date on which the object was officially registered. |
| **Last Updated** | The date of the most recent registry update. |
| **Registry Authority** | The authority responsible for maintaining the Registry Record. |
| **Change History** | References to significant administrative changes affecting the Registry Record. |
| **Administrative Notes** | Optional notes supporting registry administration. |

Conceptually, Registry Metadata is represented as follows:

```text
Registry Metadata
        │
        ├── Record Version
        ├── Record Status
        ├── Registration Date
        ├── Last Updated
        ├── Registry Authority
        ├── Change History
        └── Administrative Notes
```

Registry Metadata supports the governance and administration of the Canonical Asset Registry by providing historical and operational context for each Registry Record. It exists independently of the Canonical Asset Object itself and serves only as administrative information.

By maintaining standardized Registry Metadata, CWC-CAR ensures that every Registry Record remains traceable, maintainable, and consistently governed while preserving the stability and permanence of every Canonical Asset Object registered within the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System.

---

# Appendix B — Object Classification Taxonomy

The **Object Classification Taxonomy** defines the canonical classification framework used by the **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)** to organize Canonical Asset Objects into a consistent and scalable architectural hierarchy.

The purpose of the taxonomy is to provide a standardized vocabulary for classifying Canonical Asset Objects according to their architectural role rather than their implementation, documentation, or visual representation. By maintaining a common classification system, CWC-CAR enables consistent organization, navigation, discovery, and governance across the Canonical Asset System.

The taxonomy supports the Registry Schema by providing the standardized classification values referenced within the **Object Classification** section of every Registry Record.

Object classification follows a hierarchical model:

```text
Domain
   │
   ▼
Category
   │
   ▼
Type
   │
   ▼
Subtype
```

Each level of the taxonomy provides a progressively more specific classification while preserving the identity established by the Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID).

The Object Classification Taxonomy is independent of object identity. A Canonical Asset Object may be reclassified as the Canonical Asset System evolves without changing its Registry Record or assigned Object ID.

The following sections define the official classification taxonomy maintained by CWC-CAR.

---

## B.1 Domain

A **Domain** is the highest level of classification within the **Object Classification Taxonomy**. It represents the broadest architectural grouping of Canonical Asset Objects registered in the **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)**.

The purpose of a Domain is to organize Canonical Asset Objects into major knowledge areas that reflect their primary architectural responsibility within the Canonical Asset System. Domains provide the top-level structure for the registry and establish the foundation upon which Categories, Types, and Subtypes are organized.

Every Canonical Asset Object shall belong to exactly one primary Domain.

A Domain serves to:

- Organize the registry into major architectural areas.
- Establish the highest level of classification.
- Improve registry navigation and discoverability.
- Support long-term scalability of the Canonical Asset System.
- Provide the foundation for lower-level classifications.

Domains classify objects according to their architectural purpose rather than their implementation, ownership, or operational use.

Conceptually, the classification hierarchy begins as follows:

```text
Domain
   │
   ├── Category
   │      ├── Type
   │      │      └── Subtype
   │      └── ...
   └── ...
```

The official set of Domains is maintained by CWC-CAR and may evolve as the Canonical Asset System expands. The introduction of new Domains or the refinement of existing Domains shall not affect the canonical identity of previously registered Canonical Asset Objects.

Domains provide organizational context only. They shall never modify, replace, or redefine the Canonical Object Identifier assigned to a registered object.

---

## B.3 Type

A **Type** is the third level of classification within the **Object Classification Taxonomy**. It identifies the specific architectural nature of a Canonical Asset Object within its assigned Category.

The purpose of a Type is to distinguish Canonical Asset Objects that share the same functional grouping but represent different architectural concepts. Type classification provides the primary level at which registered objects are differentiated within the Canonical Asset Registry.

Every Canonical Asset Object shall be assigned exactly one Type.

A Type serves to:

- Identify the specific architectural nature of a Canonical Asset Object.
- Differentiate objects within the same Category.
- Improve registry precision and discoverability.
- Support consistent registration and governance.
- Provide the foundation for optional Subtype specialization.

Types describe what a Canonical Asset Object fundamentally represents within the Canonical Asset System. They do not describe implementation details, production methods, governance policies, or visual characteristics.

Conceptually, the classification hierarchy is represented as follows:

```text
Domain
   │
   ▼
Category
   │
   ▼
Type
   │
   └── Subtype
```

The official list of Types is maintained by CWC-CAR as part of the Object Classification Taxonomy. New Types may be introduced to support the continued growth of the Canonical Asset System, provided that existing Registry Records remain stable and Canonical Object Identifiers are preserved.

The assignment, modification, or refinement of a Type shall never affect the canonical identity of a registered object. Type classification exists solely to improve the structural organization and semantic clarity of the registry.

---

## B.4 Subtype

A **Subtype** is the fourth and most specific level of classification within the **Object Classification Taxonomy**. It provides an optional specialization of a Type by distinguishing Canonical Asset Objects that share the same architectural type but represent more specific conceptual variations.

The purpose of a Subtype is to increase the precision of object classification without affecting the canonical identity of the registered object. Subtypes support detailed organization, semantic clarity, and future extensibility while preserving the stability of the Registry Record and its assigned Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID).

A Canonical Asset Object may be assigned a Subtype when additional specialization is necessary. If no meaningful specialization exists, the object may remain classified solely by its Type.

A Subtype serves to:

- Refine the classification of a Canonical Asset Object.
- Distinguish specialized variations within the same Type.
- Improve registry precision and semantic consistency.
- Support future expansion of the Canonical Asset System.
- Enhance discoverability without altering object identity.

Subtypes describe increasingly specific conceptual distinctions. They shall never be used to represent implementation details, document versions, production workflows, ownership, or lifecycle states.

Conceptually, the classification hierarchy is represented as follows:

```text
Domain
   │
   ▼
Category
   │
   ▼
Type
   │
   ▼
Subtype
```

The official list of Subtypes is maintained by CWC-CAR as part of the Object Classification Taxonomy. Additional Subtypes may be introduced as new architectural concepts emerge, provided that existing classifications remain stable and Canonical Object Identifiers are preserved.

The assignment, modification, or removal of a Subtype shall never change the identity of a Canonical Asset Object. Subtypes provide organizational refinement only and do not redefine the architectural concept represented by the registered object.

---

# Appendix C — Relationship Types

The **Relationship Types** appendix defines the canonical taxonomy of relationships that may exist between **Canonical Asset Objects** registered within the **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)**.

The purpose of this taxonomy is to establish a standardized vocabulary for describing how registered objects are connected throughout the Canonical Asset System. By using consistent relationship definitions, CWC-CAR ensures that architectural connections remain explicit, traceable, interoperable, and semantically consistent.

Relationship Types describe the nature of an association between Canonical Asset Objects. They do not establish identity, ownership, specification, implementation, or governance unless explicitly defined by their respective canonical projects.

Every relationship recorded within CWC-CAR shall use one of the standardized Relationship Types defined in this appendix.

Relationship Types support:

- Architectural navigation
- Dependency analysis
- Registry traceability
- Knowledge integration
- Semantic consistency
- Long-term interoperability

Conceptually, relationships are represented as follows:

```text
Canonical Asset Object
          │
          ▼
 Relationship Type
          │
          ▼
Canonical Asset Object
```

The following sections define the official Relationship Types recognized by the Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry.

---

## C.1 Parent Of

**Parent Of** is a canonical relationship indicating that a Canonical Asset Object serves as the immediate parent of one or more subordinate Canonical Asset Objects within the architectural hierarchy of the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System.

The purpose of the **Parent Of** relationship is to establish hierarchical organization while preserving the independence and identity of each registered object. It defines structural containment rather than ownership or implementation.

A Parent Of relationship indicates that:

- The source object occupies a higher position in the architectural hierarchy.
- The target object is a direct child of the source object.
- Both objects maintain independent Canonical Object Identifiers (Object IDs).
- The relationship provides structural context only.

Conceptually, the relationship is represented as follows:

```text
Parent Object
      │
      ├── Parent Of
      ▼
Child Object
```

The existence of a Parent Of relationship shall not imply that the parent object defines, controls, or replaces the identity of the child object. Each Canonical Asset Object remains independently registered within CWC-CAR.

Parent Of relationships support hierarchical navigation, architectural organization, and traceability throughout the Canonical Asset Registry while preserving the principle of **One Object, One Identifier**.

---

## C.2 Child Of

**Child Of** is a canonical relationship indicating that a Canonical Asset Object exists as the immediate subordinate of another Canonical Asset Object within the architectural hierarchy of the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System.

The purpose of the **Child Of** relationship is to identify the immediate parent context of a registered object while preserving the independence, identity, and authority of both the parent and child objects.

A Child Of relationship indicates that:

- The source object occupies a subordinate position within the architectural hierarchy.
- The target object is the immediate parent of the source object.
- Both objects retain independent Canonical Object Identifiers (Object IDs).
- The relationship establishes structural hierarchy without affecting object identity.

Conceptually, the relationship is represented as follows:

```text
Child Object
      │
      ├── Child Of
      ▼
Parent Object
```

A Child Of relationship provides architectural context only. It shall not imply inheritance of identity, ownership, governance authority, implementation, or lifecycle. Every Canonical Asset Object remains an independently registered entity within CWC-CAR.

The Parent Of and Child Of relationships are complementary representations of the same hierarchical connection and shall remain logically consistent throughout the Canonical Asset Registry.

By maintaining explicit Child Of relationships, CWC-CAR enables reliable hierarchical navigation, structural traceability, and semantic consistency while preserving the principle that every Canonical Asset Object possesses one permanent and independent Canonical Object Identifier.

---

## C.3 Depends On

**Depends On** is a canonical relationship indicating that a Canonical Asset Object requires another Canonical Asset Object in order to exist, function, or be interpreted correctly within the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System.

The purpose of the **Depends On** relationship is to explicitly record architectural dependencies between registered objects while preserving the independence and identity of each Canonical Asset Object.

A Depends On relationship indicates that:

- The source object requires the target object as a prerequisite.
- The target object provides foundational support for the source object.
- Both objects maintain independent Canonical Object Identifiers (Object IDs).
- The dependency represents an architectural relationship rather than ownership or hierarchy.

Conceptually, the relationship is represented as follows:

```text
Dependent Object
        │
        ├── Depends On
        ▼
Required Object
```

A Depends On relationship does not imply that the required object is the parent, owner, or controller of the dependent object. Likewise, removing a dependency does not alter the canonical identity of either object. Dependencies describe architectural reliance only.

Multiple dependencies may exist for a single Canonical Asset Object. Likewise, one Canonical Asset Object may serve as a dependency for multiple other registered objects.

The Depends On relationship enables architectural analysis, dependency mapping, impact assessment, and knowledge traceability while maintaining the principle that every Canonical Asset Object remains independently identifiable and permanently registered within CWC-CAR.

---

## C.4 Referenced By

**Referenced By** is a canonical relationship indicating that a Canonical Asset Object is formally referenced by one or more other Canonical Asset Objects within the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System.

The purpose of the **Referenced By** relationship is to record explicit usage of a Canonical Asset Object by other registered objects while preserving the independence and authority of each object's canonical identity.

A Referenced By relationship indicates that:

- The target object is formally referenced by the source object.
- The reference establishes a semantic connection without creating ownership or dependency.
- Both objects retain independent Canonical Object Identifiers (Object IDs).
- The relationship supports traceability and interoperability across the Canonical Asset System.

Conceptually, the relationship is represented as follows:

```text
Referencing Object
         │
         ├── References
         ▼
Referenced Object
```

From the perspective of the referenced object, the inverse relationship is represented as:

```text
Referenced Object
         │
         ├── Referenced By
         ▼
Referencing Object
```

A Referenced By relationship does not imply inheritance, dependency, hierarchy, ownership, or implementation. It merely records that another Canonical Asset Object explicitly references the registered object as part of its canonical definition.

A Canonical Asset Object may be referenced by multiple registered objects simultaneously. Likewise, a single object may reference numerous Canonical Asset Objects throughout the registry.

By maintaining explicit Referenced By relationships, CWC-CAR enables comprehensive cross-referencing, bidirectional navigation, impact analysis, and semantic traceability while preserving the independence and permanence of every Canonical Asset Object.

---

## C.5 References

**References** is a canonical relationship indicating that a Canonical Asset Object explicitly cites or utilizes another Canonical Asset Object as part of its canonical definition within the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System.

The purpose of the **References** relationship is to establish a unidirectional semantic link from one registered object to another. It records that the source object relies upon, cites, or incorporates information from the target object without affecting the identity, authority, or lifecycle of either object.

A References relationship indicates that:

- The source object explicitly references the target object.
- The relationship establishes a semantic association rather than a structural dependency.
- Both objects maintain independent Canonical Object Identifiers (Object IDs).
- The referenced object remains fully independent of the referencing object.

Conceptually, the relationship is represented as follows:

```text
Source Object
      │
      ├── References
      ▼
Target Object
```

A References relationship does not imply ownership, hierarchy, composition, implementation, or governance. It simply records that one Canonical Asset Object makes explicit use of another within the Canonical Asset System.

Multiple References relationships may originate from a single Canonical Asset Object, enabling comprehensive knowledge integration while preserving the independence of every Registry Record.

The inverse representation of this relationship is **Referenced By**, which records the relationship from the perspective of the target object.

By maintaining standardized References relationships, CWC-CAR enables consistent cross-referencing, semantic interoperability, and traceable knowledge connections across the Canonical Asset Registry while preserving the principle that every Canonical Asset Object possesses one permanent and immutable Canonical Object Identifier.

---

## C.6 Implements

**Implements** is a canonical relationship indicating that a Canonical Asset Object realizes, instantiates, or operationalizes another Canonical Asset Object within the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System.

The purpose of the **Implements** relationship is to establish a formal connection between an abstract or governing Canonical Asset Object and another object that carries its principles, structure, or definition into practical application. This relationship preserves the distinction between conceptual authority and operational realization.

An Implements relationship indicates that:

- The source object realizes or operationalizes the target object.
- The target object defines the conceptual foundation that is being implemented.
- Both objects retain independent Canonical Object Identifiers (Object IDs).
- The implementation relationship does not imply ownership or identity inheritance.

Conceptually, the relationship is represented as follows:

```text
Implementation Object
         │
         ├── Implements
         ▼
Canonical Definition
```

An Implements relationship shall not imply that the implementation object becomes part of, replaces, or inherits the identity of the implemented object. Each Canonical Asset Object remains independently registered and governed within CWC-CAR.

A Canonical Asset Object may implement multiple canonical definitions where appropriate. Likewise, a single canonical definition may be implemented by multiple Canonical Asset Objects.

Examples of valid implementation relationships include:

- A template implementing a canonical specification.
- A production artifact implementing a design standard.
- A reference sheet implementing a registered visual component.
- A procedural document implementing a governance framework.

By maintaining standardized Implements relationships, CWC-CAR clearly distinguishes between canonical definitions and their realizations, enabling architectural traceability while preserving the independence and permanence of every Canonical Asset Object.

---

## C.7 Composed Of

**Composed Of** is a canonical relationship indicating that a Canonical Asset Object is structurally composed of one or more other Canonical Asset Objects. This relationship describes architectural composition while preserving the independent identity of each constituent object within the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System.

The purpose of the **Composed Of** relationship is to represent structural composition without implying identity inheritance, ownership, or implementation. It enables complex Canonical Asset Objects to be expressed as assemblies of smaller, independently registered objects.

A Composed Of relationship indicates that:

- The source object is structurally composed of one or more component objects.
- Each component object contributes to the overall composition.
- Every participating object retains its own Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID).
- The composition establishes structural organization only.

Conceptually, the relationship is represented as follows:

```text
Composite Object
        │
        ├── Composed Of
        ├──────────────► Component A
        ├──────────────► Component B
        └──────────────► Component C
```

A Composed Of relationship does not imply that component objects lose their independence or become inseparable from the composite object. Each component remains an independently registered Canonical Asset Object that may participate in other relationships throughout the Canonical Asset System.

Likewise, a component object may be reused as part of multiple composite objects, provided that such reuse is consistent with the canonical architecture.

The inverse representation of this relationship is **Part Of**, which records the relationship from the perspective of the component object.

By maintaining standardized Composed Of relationships, CWC-CAR enables modular architectural modeling, component reuse, and structural traceability while preserving the permanence and independence of every Canonical Asset Object.

---

## C.9 Owned By

**Owned By** is a canonical relationship indicating that a Canonical Asset Object is under the stewardship of another Canonical Asset Object that serves as its authoritative owner within the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System.

The purpose of the **Owned By** relationship is to explicitly identify the object responsible for the governance, maintenance, or custodianship of another Canonical Asset Object. This relationship establishes administrative responsibility without affecting the canonical identity or architectural independence of either object.

An Owned By relationship indicates that:

- The source object is governed or maintained by the target object.
- The target object serves as the authoritative owner or steward.
- Both objects retain independent Canonical Object Identifiers (Object IDs).
- Ownership represents governance responsibility rather than structural composition or identity inheritance.

Conceptually, the relationship is represented as follows:

```text
Managed Object
       │
       ├── Owned By
       ▼
Owner Object
```

An Owned By relationship shall not imply that the owner object becomes part of, implements, composes, or defines the identity of the managed object. The Canonical Object Identifier assigned to each registered object remains permanent and independent.

A Canonical Asset Object may own multiple registered objects. Likewise, every registered object shall have no more than one primary owner within the registry, ensuring clear governance responsibility and avoiding ambiguity.

The inverse representation of this relationship is **Owns**, which records ownership from the perspective of the governing object.

By maintaining standardized Owned By relationships, CWC-CAR enables clear governance, accountability, and administrative traceability while preserving the independence and permanence of every Canonical Asset Object.

---

## C.10 Owns

**Owns** is a canonical relationship indicating that a Canonical Asset Object serves as the authoritative owner or steward of one or more other Canonical Asset Objects within the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System.

The purpose of the **Owns** relationship is to establish clear governance responsibility for registered Canonical Asset Objects. It identifies which object is responsible for the administration, maintenance, or custodianship of another object while preserving the independent identity and authority of both parties.

An Owns relationship indicates that:

- The source object is the authoritative owner of the target object.
- The target object is governed or maintained by the source object.
- Both objects retain independent Canonical Object Identifiers (Object IDs).
- Ownership represents administrative responsibility rather than structural composition, implementation, or identity inheritance.

Conceptually, the relationship is represented as follows:

```text
Owner Object
      │
      ├── Owns
      ▼
Managed Object
```

An Owns relationship shall not imply that the owned object becomes part of the owner object or derives its canonical identity from it. Ownership defines governance responsibility only and has no effect on the permanence or independence of a Canonical Object Identifier.

A single Canonical Asset Object may own multiple registered objects. This enables clear governance structures while ensuring that each managed object remains independently registered within CWC-CAR.

The inverse representation of this relationship is **Owned By**, which records the same governance relationship from the perspective of the managed object.

By maintaining standardized Owns relationships, CWC-CAR provides explicit governance traceability, administrative clarity, and consistent stewardship across the Canonical Asset Registry while preserving the principle that every Canonical Asset Object possesses one unique, permanent, and independent canonical identity.

---

## C.11 Uses

**Uses** is a canonical relationship indicating that a Canonical Asset Object makes use of another Canonical Asset Object as part of its normal operation, definition, composition, or application within the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System.

The purpose of the **Uses** relationship is to record functional utilization between registered Canonical Asset Objects while preserving the independence and canonical identity of each object. Unlike **Depends On**, a Uses relationship indicates utilization rather than architectural necessity.

A Uses relationship indicates that:

- The source object utilizes the target object.
- The target object provides functionality, content, structure, or support to the source object.
- Both objects retain independent Canonical Object Identifiers (Object IDs).
- The relationship represents functional usage rather than ownership, hierarchy, or implementation.

Conceptually, the relationship is represented as follows:

```text
Using Object
      │
      ├── Uses
      ▼
Used Object
```

A Uses relationship does not imply dependency, ownership, inheritance, composition, or governance. The used object remains an independently registered Canonical Asset Object and may simultaneously be used by multiple other registered objects.

Likewise, a Canonical Asset Object may use multiple registered objects without affecting its Registry Record or Canonical Object Identifier.

The inverse representation of this relationship is **Used By**, which records the relationship from the perspective of the utilized object.

By maintaining standardized Uses relationships, CWC-CAR enables functional traceability, architectural transparency, and semantic consistency while preserving the permanence and independence of every Canonical Asset Object.

---

## C.12 Used By

**Used By** is a canonical relationship indicating that a Canonical Asset Object is functionally utilized by one or more other Canonical Asset Objects within the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System. It represents the inverse perspective of the **Uses** relationship.

The purpose of the **Used By** relationship is to identify all registered Canonical Asset Objects that make use of a particular Canonical Asset Object. This relationship provides visibility into asset utilization while preserving the independence and canonical identity of every participating object.

A Used By relationship indicates that:

- The target object is functionally utilized by the source object.
- The utilized object remains independent of the objects that use it.
- Both objects retain independent Canonical Object Identifiers (Object IDs).
- The relationship records functional utilization without implying ownership, dependency, or implementation.

Conceptually, the relationship is represented as follows:

```text
Used Object
      │
      ├── Used By
      ▼
Using Object
```

A Used By relationship shall not imply that the utilized object is owned by, composed within, or dependent upon the objects that use it. Functional utilization is independent of architectural hierarchy, governance, lifecycle, and object identity.

A Canonical Asset Object may be used by multiple registered objects simultaneously. Recording these relationships enables impact analysis, asset discovery, and architectural traceability throughout the Canonical Asset Registry.

The inverse representation of this relationship is **Uses**, which records the relationship from the perspective of the object performing the utilization.

By maintaining standardized Used By relationships, CWC-CAR strengthens registry transparency, improves knowledge navigation, and supports comprehensive asset analysis while preserving the permanence, uniqueness, and independence of every Canonical Asset Object.

---

## C.13 Extends

**Extends** is a canonical relationship indicating that a Canonical Asset Object builds upon, specializes, or expands another Canonical Asset Object while preserving the independent identity of both objects within the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System.

The purpose of the **Extends** relationship is to represent architectural specialization or incremental evolution without replacing, duplicating, or inheriting the canonical identity of the referenced object.

An Extends relationship indicates that:

- The source object expands or specializes the target object.
- The target object provides the foundational concept upon which the source object is based.
- Both objects retain independent Canonical Object Identifiers (Object IDs).
- The relationship establishes conceptual extension rather than ownership, composition, or implementation.

Conceptually, the relationship is represented as follows:

```text
Extended Object
       │
       ├── Extends
       ▼
Base Object
```

An Extends relationship shall not imply that the extended object inherits the identity, Registry Record, or lifecycle of the base object. Each Canonical Asset Object remains independently registered and governed within CWC-CAR.

Multiple Canonical Asset Objects may extend the same base object, enabling the Canonical Asset System to evolve through specialization while maintaining a stable architectural foundation.

The inverse representation of this relationship is **Extended By**, which records the relationship from the perspective of the base object.

By maintaining standardized Extends relationships, CWC-CAR supports controlled architectural evolution, conceptual specialization, and semantic traceability while preserving the permanence, uniqueness, and independence of every Canonical Asset Object.

---

## C.14 Extended By

**Extended By** is a canonical relationship indicating that a Canonical Asset Object serves as the foundational object upon which one or more other Canonical Asset Objects are conceptually expanded or specialized within the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System. It represents the inverse perspective of the **Extends** relationship.

The purpose of the **Extended By** relationship is to identify Canonical Asset Objects that derive additional concepts, capabilities, or specializations from a registered object while preserving the independent identity of every participating object.

An Extended By relationship indicates that:

- The target object serves as the conceptual foundation.
- The source object extends or specializes the target object.
- Both objects retain independent Canonical Object Identifiers (Object IDs).
- The relationship records conceptual evolution without implying identity inheritance or replacement.

Conceptually, the relationship is represented as follows:

```text
Base Object
      │
      ├── Extended By
      ▼
Extended Object
```

An Extended By relationship does not imply ownership, composition, implementation, governance, or lifecycle dependency. It simply records that another Canonical Asset Object has been developed as a conceptual extension of the registered object.

A Canonical Asset Object may be extended by multiple registered objects simultaneously. Recording these relationships enables architectural evolution to remain explicit, traceable, and semantically consistent across the Canonical Asset Registry.

The inverse representation of this relationship is **Extends**, which records the relationship from the perspective of the extending object.

By maintaining standardized Extended By relationships, CWC-CAR supports controlled architectural evolution, knowledge traceability, and semantic consistency while preserving the permanence, uniqueness, and independence of every Canonical Asset Object.

---

## C.15 Replaces

**Replaces** is a canonical relationship indicating that a Canonical Asset Object supersedes another Canonical Asset Object as its canonical successor within the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System.

The purpose of the **Replaces** relationship is to preserve historical continuity while allowing the Canonical Asset System to evolve. Rather than modifying or deleting an existing Canonical Asset Object, a new registered object may explicitly replace an earlier one, ensuring that the evolution of canonical assets remains fully traceable.

A Replaces relationship indicates that:

- The source object is the canonical successor of the target object.
- The target object is superseded but remains historically registered.
- Both objects retain independent Canonical Object Identifiers (Object IDs).
- The relationship records canonical succession rather than identity inheritance.

Conceptually, the relationship is represented as follows:

```text
Successor Object
        │
        ├── Replaces
        ▼
Previous Object
```

A Replaces relationship shall not alter or invalidate the Canonical Object Identifier of either object. The replaced object remains a permanent part of the Canonical Asset Registry for historical reference and traceability.

The existence of a Replaces relationship does not imply that the predecessor object is deleted or merged. Instead, it establishes a documented transition from one Canonical Asset Object to another while preserving both Registry Records.

The inverse representation of this relationship is **Replaced By**, which records the relationship from the perspective of the superseded object.

By maintaining standardized Replaces relationships, CWC-CAR supports controlled canonical evolution, historical continuity, and complete architectural traceability while preserving the permanence and integrity of every Canonical Asset Object.

---

## C.16 Replaced By

**Replaced By** is a canonical relationship indicating that a Canonical Asset Object has been superseded by another Canonical Asset Object within the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System. It represents the inverse perspective of the **Replaces** relationship.

The purpose of the **Replaced By** relationship is to preserve historical continuity by explicitly identifying the canonical successor of a registered object. This relationship allows the Canonical Asset Registry to maintain a complete history of architectural evolution without modifying or deleting historical Registry Records.

A Replaced By relationship indicates that:

- The source object has been superseded by the target object.
- The target object serves as the canonical successor.
- Both objects retain independent Canonical Object Identifiers (Object IDs).
- The relationship records canonical succession while preserving historical traceability.

Conceptually, the relationship is represented as follows:

```text
Previous Object
        │
        ├── Replaced By
        ▼
Successor Object
```

A Replaced By relationship shall not invalidate, merge, or remove the Registry Record of the superseded object. Historical Registry Records remain permanently registered within CWC-CAR to preserve canonical continuity and enable long-term traceability.

The relationship also does not imply identity inheritance. The successor object receives its own Canonical Object Identifier and exists as an independent Canonical Asset Object within the registry.

The inverse representation of this relationship is **Replaces**, which records the relationship from the perspective of the successor object.

By maintaining standardized Replaced By relationships, CWC-CAR preserves the historical evolution of the Canonical Asset System while ensuring that every Canonical Asset Object remains uniquely identifiable, permanently registered, and architecturally traceable.

---

## C.17 Equivalent To

**Equivalent To** is a canonical relationship indicating that two or more Canonical Asset Objects represent the same architectural concept in different contexts while remaining independently registered within the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System.

The purpose of the **Equivalent To** relationship is to explicitly record semantic equivalence between Canonical Asset Objects without merging their identities or Registry Records. This relationship supports interoperability across domains, registries, and canonical projects where distinct objects serve equivalent conceptual roles.

An Equivalent To relationship indicates that:

- The participating objects are semantically equivalent.
- Each object retains its own Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID).
- Equivalence does not imply ownership, dependency, hierarchy, implementation, or replacement.
- The relationship preserves independent registration while acknowledging conceptual equivalence.

Conceptually, the relationship is represented as follows:

```text
Canonical Asset Object A
          │
          ├── Equivalent To
          ▼
Canonical Asset Object B
```

An Equivalent To relationship shall never be interpreted as identity duplication. Each Canonical Asset Object continues to exist as an independent Registry Record with its own lifecycle, governance, and canonical references.

The relationship is symmetrical. If Object A is **Equivalent To** Object B, then Object B is likewise **Equivalent To** Object A.

Equivalent To relationships are particularly valuable when architectural concepts appear in multiple domains or canonical contexts but require separate registration due to differences in governance, scope, or lifecycle.

By maintaining standardized Equivalent To relationships, CWC-CAR supports semantic interoperability, cross-domain consistency, and knowledge integration while preserving the uniqueness, permanence, and independence of every Canonical Asset Object.

---

## C.18 Related To

**Related To** is a canonical relationship indicating that two Canonical Asset Objects share a meaningful architectural association that is not sufficiently described by any other specialized Relationship Type within the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System.

The purpose of the **Related To** relationship is to provide a general semantic association between Canonical Asset Objects while preserving the independence, identity, and governance of each registered object. It serves as a neutral relationship for architectural connections that do not represent hierarchy, dependency, implementation, composition, ownership, or succession.

A Related To relationship indicates that:

- The participating objects possess a recognized architectural association.
- Neither object depends upon, owns, implements, extends, or replaces the other.
- Both objects retain independent Canonical Object Identifiers (Object IDs).
- The relationship provides contextual association only.

Conceptually, the relationship is represented as follows:

```text
Canonical Asset Object A
          │
          ├── Related To
          ▼
Canonical Asset Object B
```

A Related To relationship shall not be used when a more specific Relationship Type accurately describes the connection between two Canonical Asset Objects. Specialized relationships such as **Parent Of**, **Depends On**, **References**, **Implements**, or **Composed Of** shall always take precedence where applicable.

The relationship is inherently symmetrical. If Object A is **Related To** Object B, then Object B is likewise **Related To** Object A.

Related To relationships improve architectural discoverability, semantic navigation, and contextual understanding while preserving the independence and permanence of every Canonical Asset Object.

As the most general relationship within the Canonical Asset Registry, Related To should be used only when no other standardized Relationship Type provides a more precise representation of the association.

---

## C.19 Associated With

**Associated With** is a canonical relationship indicating that two Canonical Asset Objects maintain a recognized architectural association without implying hierarchy, dependency, implementation, ownership, composition, succession, or semantic equivalence within the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System.

The purpose of the **Associated With** relationship is to record meaningful contextual connections that contribute to architectural understanding while remaining intentionally neutral with respect to governance, structure, and behavior.

An Associated With relationship indicates that:

- The participating objects are contextually connected.
- The association does not establish a structural or operational dependency.
- Both objects retain independent Canonical Object Identifiers (Object IDs).
- The relationship exists solely to improve architectural context and discoverability.

Conceptually, the relationship is represented as follows:

```text
Canonical Asset Object A
          │
          ├── Associated With
          ▼
Canonical Asset Object B
```

An Associated With relationship shall not be interpreted as evidence of ownership, implementation, inheritance, composition, dependency, or replacement. If a more specific Relationship Type accurately describes the connection between two Canonical Asset Objects, that specialized relationship shall be used instead.

The relationship is symmetrical. If Object A is **Associated With** Object B, then Object B is likewise **Associated With** Object A.

Associated With relationships are particularly useful for recording contextual, thematic, or conceptual associations that improve navigation and understanding across the Canonical Asset Registry while preserving the architectural independence of every registered object.

By maintaining standardized Associated With relationships, CWC-CAR enriches semantic connectivity and architectural discoverability without compromising the permanence, uniqueness, or integrity of Canonical Asset Object identities.

---

## C.20 Derived From

**Derived From** is a canonical relationship indicating that a Canonical Asset Object originates conceptually from another Canonical Asset Object while remaining an independent architectural entity within the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System.

The purpose of the **Derived From** relationship is to preserve conceptual lineage by recording the origin of a Canonical Asset Object without implying inheritance of identity, ownership, implementation, or governance.

A Derived From relationship indicates that:

- The source object originated from the target object.
- The target object serves as the conceptual origin or source.
- Both objects retain independent Canonical Object Identifiers (Object IDs).
- The relationship records conceptual lineage rather than canonical succession.

Conceptually, the relationship is represented as follows:

```text
Derived Object
      │
      ├── Derived From
      ▼
Source Object
```

A Derived From relationship shall not imply that the derived object replaces, extends, or implements the source object. Likewise, the source object remains unaffected by subsequent evolution of the derived object. Each Canonical Asset Object maintains its own Registry Record, lifecycle, governance, and canonical references.

Multiple Canonical Asset Objects may be derived from the same source object. Similarly, a derived object may itself become the source for additional derived objects, enabling the registry to preserve complete conceptual lineage across multiple generations.

The inverse representation of this relationship is **Derives**, which records the relationship from the perspective of the originating object.

By maintaining standardized Derived From relationships, CWC-CAR preserves conceptual provenance, supports architectural evolution, and enables comprehensive lineage tracking while maintaining the permanence, uniqueness, and independence of every Canonical Asset Object.

---

## C.21 Derives

**Derives** is a canonical relationship indicating that a Canonical Asset Object serves as the conceptual origin from which one or more other Canonical Asset Objects are derived within the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System. It represents the inverse perspective of the **Derived From** relationship.

The purpose of the **Derives** relationship is to explicitly identify the downstream Canonical Asset Objects that originate from a registered object while preserving the independent identity, governance, and lifecycle of every participating object.

A Derives relationship indicates that:

- The source object serves as the conceptual origin.
- The target object is conceptually derived from the source object.
- Both objects retain independent Canonical Object Identifiers (Object IDs).
- The relationship records conceptual lineage without implying ownership, implementation, inheritance, or canonical succession.

Conceptually, the relationship is represented as follows:

```text
Source Object
      │
      ├── Derives
      ▼
Derived Object
```

A Derives relationship shall not imply that the derived object inherits the identity, Registry Record, governance, or lifecycle of the originating object. Every Canonical Asset Object remains independently registered and permanently identifiable within CWC-CAR.

A single Canonical Asset Object may derive multiple downstream objects, allowing the Canonical Asset Registry to represent branching conceptual evolution while maintaining complete architectural traceability.

The inverse representation of this relationship is **Derived From**, which records the same conceptual lineage from the perspective of the derived object.

By maintaining standardized Derives relationships, CWC-CAR preserves conceptual provenance, supports knowledge evolution, and enables comprehensive lineage analysis while maintaining the permanence, uniqueness, and independence of every Canonical Asset Object.

---

## C.22 Derived Into

**Derived Into** is a canonical relationship indicating that a Canonical Asset Object has evolved or been transformed into one or more new Canonical Asset Objects while preserving the historical continuity of the originating object within the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System.

The purpose of the **Derived Into** relationship is to record forward conceptual evolution from an originating Canonical Asset Object to its subsequent derivatives. Unlike **Replaces**, which represents canonical succession, **Derived Into** represents conceptual branching in which the originating object continues to exist independently.

A Derived Into relationship indicates that:

- The source object serves as the conceptual origin.
- One or more target objects are derived from the source object.
- All participating objects retain independent Canonical Object Identifiers (Object IDs).
- The originating object remains a valid Canonical Asset Object after derivation.

Conceptually, the relationship is represented as follows:

```text
Origin Object
      │
      ├── Derived Into
      ├──────────────► Derived Object A
      ├──────────────► Derived Object B
      └──────────────► Derived Object C
```

A Derived Into relationship shall not imply replacement, deprecation, ownership, implementation, or identity inheritance. It records only that new Canonical Asset Objects have been conceptually derived from an existing object while preserving the independent identity and lifecycle of every Registry Record.

Multiple Derived Into relationships may originate from the same Canonical Asset Object, enabling the Canonical Asset Registry to represent branching architectural evolution and conceptual diversification over time.

The inverse representation of this relationship is **Derived From**, which records the same conceptual lineage from the perspective of each derived object.

By maintaining standardized Derived Into relationships, CWC-CAR preserves forward conceptual lineage, supports architectural evolution, and enables comprehensive traceability across generations of Canonical Asset Objects while maintaining the permanence, uniqueness, and independence of every registered object.

---

## C.23 Originates From

**Originates From** is a canonical relationship indicating that a Canonical Asset Object traces its conceptual origin to another Canonical Asset Object within the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System. This relationship identifies the earliest recognized source of an object's conceptual existence while preserving the independent identity of all participating objects.

The purpose of the **Originates From** relationship is to distinguish conceptual origin from other forms of architectural relationships such as implementation, extension, dependency, or replacement. It provides historical provenance without implying ownership, hierarchy, or lifecycle succession.

An Originates From relationship indicates that:

- The source object originated conceptually from the target object.
- The target object represents the recognized conceptual origin.
- Both objects retain independent Canonical Object Identifiers (Object IDs).
- The relationship records historical provenance rather than structural or operational dependency.

Conceptually, the relationship is represented as follows:

```text
Originated Object
        │
        ├── Originates From
        ▼
Origin Object
```

An Originates From relationship shall not imply that the originating object governs, owns, implements, extends, or replaces the originating source. Likewise, the originating source remains an independent Canonical Asset Object regardless of subsequent evolution of derived objects.

A Canonical Asset Object may originate from multiple conceptual influences where such relationships are explicitly recognized within the registry. Likewise, a single originating object may serve as the conceptual source for numerous Canonical Asset Objects.

The inverse representation of this relationship is **Originates**, which records the same provenance from the perspective of the originating object.

By maintaining standardized Originates From relationships, CWC-CAR preserves conceptual provenance, strengthens historical traceability, and supports long-term architectural understanding while maintaining the permanence, uniqueness, and independence of every Canonical Asset Object.

---

## C.24 Originates

**Originates** is a canonical relationship indicating that a Canonical Asset Object serves as the recognized conceptual origin for one or more other Canonical Asset Objects within the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System. It represents the inverse perspective of the **Originates From** relationship.

The purpose of the **Originates** relationship is to explicitly identify the Canonical Asset Objects whose conceptual existence can be traced back to a common originating object. This relationship preserves architectural provenance while maintaining the independence, identity, and governance of every participating object.

An Originates relationship indicates that:

- The source object serves as the recognized conceptual origin.
- The target object originates conceptually from the source object.
- Both objects retain independent Canonical Object Identifiers (Object IDs).
- The relationship records conceptual provenance without implying ownership, dependency, implementation, or identity inheritance.

Conceptually, the relationship is represented as follows:

```text
Origin Object
      │
      ├── Originates
      ▼
Originated Object
```

An Originates relationship shall not imply that the originating object controls, governs, replaces, or structurally contains the originated object. Every Canonical Asset Object remains independently registered within CWC-CAR with its own Registry Record, lifecycle, and canonical references.

A single Canonical Asset Object may originate multiple downstream Canonical Asset Objects. This enables the Canonical Asset Registry to preserve complete conceptual provenance while supporting branching architectural evolution across the Canonical Asset System.

The inverse representation of this relationship is **Originates From**, which records the same provenance from the perspective of the originating object.

By maintaining standardized Originates relationships, CWC-CAR provides a consistent mechanism for recording conceptual origins, preserving architectural history, and enabling comprehensive provenance analysis while maintaining the permanence, uniqueness, and independence of every Canonical Asset Object.

---

## C.25 Synchronizes With

**Synchronizes With** is a canonical relationship indicating that two or more Canonical Asset Objects are intended to remain consistent with one another throughout their respective lifecycles within the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System.

The purpose of the **Synchronizes With** relationship is to explicitly record synchronization requirements between registered Canonical Asset Objects. This relationship supports consistency across canonical assets while preserving the independent identity, governance, and lifecycle of each participating object.

A Synchronizes With relationship indicates that:

- The participating objects are expected to maintain conceptual or structural consistency.
- Changes to one object may require corresponding review or updates to related objects.
- Each object retains its own Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID).
- Synchronization does not imply ownership, dependency, replacement, implementation, or identity inheritance.

Conceptually, the relationship is represented as follows:

```text
Canonical Asset Object A
          │
          ├── Synchronizes With
          ▼
Canonical Asset Object B
```

A Synchronizes With relationship is inherently symmetrical. If Object A **Synchronizes With** Object B, then Object B likewise **Synchronizes With** Object A.

Synchronization requirements do not require simultaneous modification of participating objects. Rather, they indicate that changes to one object should be evaluated to determine whether corresponding updates are appropriate for the related object.

Examples of appropriate synchronization relationships include:

- A Canonical Asset Object and its corresponding Reference Sheet.
- A registry taxonomy and its associated specification.
- Two complementary governance objects that must remain aligned.
- Parallel canonical structures maintained in separate domains.

By maintaining standardized Synchronizes With relationships, CWC-CAR supports long-term consistency, coordinated evolution, and architectural coherence while preserving the permanence, uniqueness, and independence of every Canonical Asset Object.

---

## C.26 Synchronized By

**Synchronized By** is a canonical relationship indicating that a Canonical Asset Object is maintained in synchronization with one or more other Canonical Asset Objects within the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System. It represents the inverse perspective of the **Synchronizes With** relationship.

The purpose of the **Synchronized By** relationship is to identify the Canonical Asset Objects that participate in maintaining consistency with a registered object. This relationship improves traceability of coordinated updates while preserving the independent identity, governance, and lifecycle of every participating object.

A Synchronized By relationship indicates that:

- The target object participates in maintaining synchronization with the source object.
- Each participating object retains its own Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID).
- Synchronization represents consistency management rather than ownership, dependency, implementation, or replacement.
- All participating Registry Records remain independently governed.

Conceptually, the relationship is represented as follows:

```text
Canonical Asset Object A
          │
          ├── Synchronized By
          ▼
Canonical Asset Object B
```

A Synchronized By relationship is inherently symmetrical with **Synchronizes With**. If Object A **Synchronizes With** Object B, then Object B may record the inverse relationship as **Synchronized By** Object A.

Synchronization relationships shall not alter the canonical identity, Registry Record, lifecycle state, or governance authority of any participating object. They exist solely to preserve architectural consistency across related Canonical Asset Objects.

Typical examples include synchronization between:

- Registry Records and Canonical Specifications.
- Canonical Specifications and Reference Sheets.
- Shared taxonomies across multiple canonical registries.
- Parallel governance documents maintained under separate canonical projects.

By maintaining standardized Synchronized By relationships, CWC-CAR enables coordinated maintenance, bidirectional traceability, and long-term architectural consistency while preserving the permanence, uniqueness, and independence of every Canonical Asset Object.

---

## C.27 Conflicts With

**Conflicts With** is a canonical relationship indicating that two Canonical Asset Objects contain mutually incompatible concepts, definitions, responsibilities, or architectural roles within the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System.

The purpose of the **Conflicts With** relationship is to explicitly document known architectural conflicts while preserving the independent identity and historical integrity of every participating Canonical Asset Object. Recording conflicts enables the Canonical Asset Registry to identify incompatibilities without removing or modifying existing Registry Records.

A Conflicts With relationship indicates that:

- The participating objects cannot coexist in the same architectural context without inconsistency.
- Each object retains its own Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID).
- The conflict is explicitly recognized and documented within the registry.
- The relationship does not imply replacement, ownership, dependency, implementation, or governance authority.

Conceptually, the relationship is represented as follows:

```text
Canonical Asset Object A
          │
          ├── Conflicts With
          ▼
Canonical Asset Object B
```

A Conflicts With relationship is inherently symmetrical. If Object A **Conflicts With** Object B, then Object B likewise **Conflicts With** Object A.

The existence of a conflict does not invalidate either Canonical Asset Object. Instead, it provides architectural transparency by documenting incompatible concepts, overlapping responsibilities, or mutually exclusive canonical definitions that require governance consideration.

Typical examples include:

- Two alternative standards that cannot be applied simultaneously.
- Competing governance models.
- Mutually exclusive architectural patterns.
- Deprecated concepts that conflict with modern canonical replacements.

By maintaining standardized Conflicts With relationships, CWC-CAR supports architectural analysis, governance transparency, and informed decision-making while preserving the permanence, uniqueness, and independence of every Canonical Asset Object.

---

## C.28 Conflicted By

**Conflicted By** is a canonical relationship indicating that a Canonical Asset Object is recognized as being in conflict with one or more other Canonical Asset Objects within the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System. It represents the inverse perspective of the **Conflicts With** relationship.

The purpose of the **Conflicted By** relationship is to provide the reciprocal view of documented architectural conflicts, enabling complete bidirectional traceability while preserving the independent identity and integrity of every participating Canonical Asset Object.

A Conflicted By relationship indicates that:

- The target object has been identified as conflicting with the source object.
- Each participating object retains its own Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID).
- The relationship records architectural incompatibility without affecting the identity, lifecycle, or governance of either object.
- Both Registry Records remain independently maintained within CWC-CAR.

Conceptually, the relationship is represented as follows:

```text
Canonical Asset Object A
          │
          ├── Conflicted By
          ▼
Canonical Asset Object B
```

A Conflicted By relationship is inherently symmetrical with **Conflicts With**. If Object A **Conflicts With** Object B, then Object B may record the inverse relationship as **Conflicted By** Object A.

Recording a Conflicted By relationship does not invalidate, deprecate, replace, or merge either Canonical Asset Object. Instead, it documents the existence of a recognized architectural incompatibility, allowing governance processes and future architectural decisions to consider the conflict explicitly.

Typical examples include:

- A governance object conflicting with an alternative governance framework.
- Two incompatible taxonomy definitions.
- Mutually exclusive architectural standards.
- Canonical objects that cannot be applied simultaneously within the same architectural context.

By maintaining standardized Conflicted By relationships, CWC-CAR provides complete bidirectional conflict traceability, improves architectural transparency, and supports informed governance decisions while preserving the permanence, uniqueness, and independence of every Canonical Asset Object.

---

## C.29 Confirms

**Confirms** is a canonical relationship indicating that a Canonical Asset Object formally validates, verifies, or affirms another Canonical Asset Object within the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System.

The purpose of the **Confirms** relationship is to record explicit canonical confirmation between registered objects. This relationship supports governance, validation, and quality assurance by documenting that one Canonical Asset Object recognizes another as satisfying defined architectural, procedural, or canonical requirements.

A Confirms relationship indicates that:

- The source object formally confirms the target object.
- The confirmation represents validation rather than ownership, dependency, implementation, or replacement.
- Both objects retain independent Canonical Object Identifiers (Object IDs).
- The relationship records canonical verification while preserving the independence of each Registry Record.

Conceptually, the relationship is represented as follows:

```text
Confirming Object
        │
        ├── Confirms
        ▼
Confirmed Object
```

A Confirms relationship shall not imply that the confirming object governs, owns, replaces, or composes the confirmed object. Confirmation represents recognition of compliance, correctness, completeness, or validity within an established canonical context.

Multiple Canonical Asset Objects may independently confirm the same object where multiple validation authorities or review stages exist.

The inverse representation of this relationship is **Confirmed By**, which records the relationship from the perspective of the confirmed object.

By maintaining standardized Confirms relationships, CWC-CAR provides explicit validation traceability, strengthens registry governance, and supports transparent canonical quality assurance while preserving the permanence, uniqueness, and independence of every Canonical Asset Object.

---

## C.30 Confirmed By

**Confirmed By** is a canonical relationship indicating that a Canonical Asset Object has been formally validated, verified, or affirmed by another Canonical Asset Object within the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System. It represents the inverse perspective of the **Confirms** relationship.

The purpose of the **Confirmed By** relationship is to provide explicit traceability of canonical validation from the perspective of the validated object. This relationship documents that a registered Canonical Asset Object has successfully undergone formal review, verification, or approval while preserving its independent identity and Registry Record.

A Confirmed By relationship indicates that:

- The target object has formally confirmed the source object.
- The confirmation represents validation rather than ownership, dependency, implementation, or replacement.
- Both objects retain independent Canonical Object Identifiers (Object IDs).
- The relationship records canonical verification without altering the identity or governance of either object.

Conceptually, the relationship is represented as follows:

```text
Confirmed Object
        │
        ├── Confirmed By
        ▼
Confirming Object
```

A Confirmed By relationship shall not imply that the confirming object owns, governs, implements, composes, or replaces the confirmed object. Canonical confirmation records validation only and has no effect on the permanent identity or lifecycle of either Canonical Asset Object.

A Canonical Asset Object may be confirmed by multiple independent objects where different review authorities, governance bodies, or validation processes participate in canonical verification.

The inverse representation of this relationship is **Confirms**, which records the same validation relationship from the perspective of the confirming object.

By maintaining standardized Confirmed By relationships, CWC-CAR strengthens governance transparency, validation traceability, and canonical quality assurance while preserving the permanence, uniqueness, and independence of every Canonical Asset Object.

---

# Appendix D — Registry Status

The **Registry Status** appendix defines the official registration states that may be assigned to **Registry Records** within the **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)**.

The purpose of Registry Status is to provide a standardized mechanism for indicating the current registration condition of a Canonical Asset Object throughout its lifecycle. Registry Status communicates the administrative standing of a Registry Record without affecting the object's canonical identity, Object ID, classification, or architectural relationships.

Registry Status applies exclusively to the Registry Record maintained by CWC-CAR. It does not represent the implementation status, publication status, production readiness, or operational usage of the corresponding Canonical Asset Object.

Every Registry Record shall possess exactly one current Registry Status.

The official Registry Status values defined by CWC-CAR are:

| Registry Status | Description |
|-----------------|-------------|
| **Candidate** | The object has been identified and is under evaluation for registration. |
| **Registered** | The object has been formally accepted and assigned a Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID). |
| **Active** | The Registry Record is fully active and available for canonical use. |
| **Deprecated** | The Registry Record remains valid for historical reference but is no longer recommended for new canonical usage. |
| **Archived** | The Registry Record is permanently retained for historical preservation and is no longer actively maintained. |

Conceptually, Registry Status progresses as follows:

```text
Candidate
    │
    ▼
Registered
    │
    ▼
Active
    │
    ├──────────────┐
    ▼              │
Deprecated         │
    │              │
    ▼              │
Archived ◄─────────┘
```

Changes to Registry Status shall never modify the Canonical Object Identifier or the identity of the registered Canonical Asset Object. Registry Status exists solely to communicate the current administrative state of the Registry Record.

The following sections define each Registry Status in detail.

---

## D.1 Candidate

**Candidate** is the initial Registry Status assigned to a potential Canonical Asset Object that has been identified for inclusion within the **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)** but has not yet completed the formal registration process.

The purpose of the **Candidate** status is to provide a controlled stage during which a proposed Canonical Asset Object may be evaluated for uniqueness, architectural relevance, and eligibility before receiving a permanent Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID).

A Registry Record in the Candidate status indicates that:

- The proposed object has been identified.
- Preliminary evaluation is in progress.
- Registration has not yet been approved.
- No permanent Canonical Object Identifier has been assigned.
- The object is not yet considered part of the official Canonical Asset Registry.

Conceptually, the Candidate status is represented as follows:

```text
Potential Canonical Asset Object
              │
              ▼
          Candidate
              │
      (Under Evaluation)
```

A Candidate object shall not be referenced as an official Canonical Asset Object by CWC-CAB, CWC-CRS, or any other canonical project until it has successfully progressed to the **Registered** status.

The Candidate status exists solely to support the controlled intake of new Canonical Asset Objects. It provides an evaluation stage without establishing canonical identity or registry authority.

Upon successful evaluation, a Candidate progresses to the **Registered** status, where a permanent Canonical Object Identifier is assigned and the object becomes an official member of the Canonical Asset Registry.

---

## D.2 Registered

**Registered** is the Registry Status assigned to a Canonical Asset Object that has successfully completed the formal registration process within the **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)**.

The purpose of the **Registered** status is to formally recognize the existence of a Canonical Asset Object within the Canonical Asset System. Upon entering this status, the object is assigned a permanent **Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID)** and a corresponding Registry Record is established.

A Registry Record in the Registered status indicates that:

- The object has successfully passed the registration process.
- A permanent Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID) has been assigned.
- The Registry Record has been officially created.
- The object is recognized as an official Canonical Asset Object.
- The object is eligible to be referenced by other canonical projects.

Conceptually, the Registered status is represented as follows:

```text
Candidate
     │
     ▼
Registered
     │
(Object ID Assigned)
```

The Registered status establishes the canonical identity of the object but does not imply that its specification, reference representation, implementation, or governance documentation has been completed. Those activities are managed independently by their respective canonical projects.

Once a Canonical Asset Object reaches the Registered status, its Canonical Object Identifier becomes permanent and immutable. The assigned Object ID shall never be modified, reassigned, or reused, regardless of future changes to the object's lifecycle, classification, or registry metadata.

The Registered status represents the official entry point into the Canonical Asset Registry and serves as the prerequisite for all subsequent canonical specifications, reference representations, and architectural relationships.

---

## D.3 Active

**Active** is the Registry Status assigned to a Registry Record that is fully established, maintained, and recognized as the current authoritative registration of a Canonical Asset Object within the **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)**.

The purpose of the **Active** status is to indicate that a Canonical Asset Object is in normal canonical use and serves as the current authoritative identity for its associated specifications, reference representations, and architectural relationships.

A Registry Record in the Active status indicates that:

- The Canonical Asset Object is officially recognized within the Canonical Asset System.
- The assigned Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID) is active and authoritative.
- The Registry Record is available for canonical reference by all related projects.
- The object may participate in classifications, relationships, specifications, and reference representations.
- The Registry Record is actively maintained under the governance of CWC-CAR.

Conceptually, the Active status is represented as follows:

```text
Registered
     │
     ▼
Active
     │
(Current Authoritative Registry Record)
```

The Active status represents the normal operating state of a Registry Record. While an object remains Active, its canonical identity is considered current and authoritative for all canonical activities across the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System.

Changes to specifications, documentation, governance, or reference representations shall not affect the Active status unless a formal registry decision is made. Likewise, modifications to an Active Registry Record shall never alter the assigned Canonical Object Identifier.

An Active Registry Record may eventually transition to **Deprecated** or **Archived** as the Canonical Asset System evolves. Such transitions affect only the administrative status of the Registry Record and shall never invalidate the permanent identity of the Canonical Asset Object.

---

## D.4 Deprecated

**Deprecated** is the Registry Status assigned to a Registry Record that remains part of the **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)** but is no longer recommended for new canonical use.

The purpose of the **Deprecated** status is to preserve historical continuity while guiding future development toward newer or preferred Canonical Asset Objects. Deprecation communicates that an object has been superseded, replaced, or is otherwise no longer the recommended canonical choice, without removing its Registry Record or altering its canonical identity.

A Registry Record in the Deprecated status indicates that:

- The Canonical Asset Object remains officially registered.
- The assigned Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID) remains valid and permanent.
- Existing canonical references remain historically valid.
- New canonical developments should avoid using the deprecated object where an appropriate alternative exists.
- The Registry Record is retained for historical traceability and compatibility.

Conceptually, the Deprecated status is represented as follows:

```text
Active
   │
   ▼
Deprecated
   │
(Historical Reference Retained)
```

Deprecation does not invalidate or remove a Canonical Asset Object from the Canonical Asset Registry. The Registry Record remains accessible for historical reference, architectural traceability, and compatibility with previously established canonical documents.

A Deprecated Registry Record may include references to its recommended successor through canonical relationships such as **Replaced By** or **Replaces**, enabling users to identify the current preferred Canonical Asset Object while preserving the complete historical lineage.

The Deprecated status affects only the recommended future use of a Registry Record. It shall never modify the Canonical Object Identifier, Registry Record, object classification, or historical integrity of the registered Canonical Asset Object.

---

## D.5 Archived

**Archived** is the Registry Status assigned to a Registry Record that has reached the end of its active administrative lifecycle while remaining permanently preserved within the **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)**.

The purpose of the **Archived** status is to ensure that historical Canonical Asset Objects remain available for reference, traceability, and audit purposes without participating in the active evolution of the Canonical Asset System.

A Registry Record in the Archived status indicates that:

- The Canonical Asset Object remains permanently registered.
- The assigned Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID) remains valid and immutable.
- The Registry Record is preserved as part of the permanent historical registry.
- The object is no longer actively maintained or recommended for operational canonical use.
- Historical relationships, classifications, and references remain intact.

Conceptually, the Archived status is represented as follows:

```text
Active
   │
   ├────────────► Deprecated
   │                  │
   ▼                  ▼
Archived ◄────────────┘
```

Archiving does not remove, invalidate, or recycle a Registry Record. Every Archived Canonical Asset Object continues to exist within the Canonical Asset Registry as a permanent historical record, ensuring that past canonical decisions remain fully traceable.

An Archived Registry Record shall retain its complete registration history, Canonical Object Identifier, classifications, relationships, metadata, and canonical references. These records provide continuity across versions of the Canonical Asset System and support long-term architectural governance.

The Archived status represents the final administrative state of a Registry Record. Although the object is no longer actively maintained, its canonical identity remains permanent and shall never be reassigned, modified, or reused.

By maintaining Archived Registry Records indefinitely, CWC-CAR preserves the historical integrity, traceability, and continuity of the Canonical Asset System while ensuring that every Canonical Asset Object remains permanently identifiable throughout its entire existence.

---

# Appendix E — Canonical Object Identifier Specification

The **Canonical Object Identifier Specification** defines the standards governing the creation, assignment, structure, and long-term management of **Canonical Object Identifiers (Object IDs)** within the **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)**.

The purpose of this appendix is to ensure that every Canonical Asset Object receives a permanent, unique, and stable identifier that remains valid throughout its entire lifecycle. The Object ID serves as the authoritative identity of a Canonical Asset Object and provides the foundation for interoperability, traceability, and governance across the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System.

The Object ID is independent of object names, classifications, specifications, implementations, lifecycle states, and visual representations. Changes to any of these characteristics shall never affect the assigned Object ID.

Every Canonical Object Identifier shall satisfy the following principles:

- **Uniqueness** — Each Object ID identifies one and only one Canonical Asset Object.
- **Permanence** — Once assigned, an Object ID shall never change.
- **Immutability** — Object IDs shall never be edited, reassigned, or reused.
- **Traceability** — Every Object ID shall remain permanently traceable throughout the registry.
- **Independence** — Object IDs shall not encode implementation details, lifecycle states, ownership, or version information.
- **Scalability** — The identification system shall support the continued growth of the Canonical Asset System without structural modification.

Conceptually, the role of the Canonical Object Identifier is represented as follows:

```text
Canonical Asset Object
          │
          ▼
Canonical Object Identifier
          │
          ├── Registry Record
          ├── Canonical Specification
          ├── Reference Representation
          └── Registry Relationships
```

This appendix defines the official rules governing Object ID structure, assignment, uniqueness, maintenance, and long-term preservation.

The following sections specify each aspect of the Canonical Object Identifier in detail.

---

## E.1 Object ID Format

The **Object ID Format** defines the standardized syntax used to represent every **Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID)** within the **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)**.

The purpose of the Object ID Format is to establish a consistent, machine-readable, and human-readable identification system that uniquely identifies every Canonical Asset Object throughout its entire lifecycle.

The Object ID serves solely as the permanent identity of a Canonical Asset Object. It is not intended to encode architectural meaning, implementation details, lifecycle information, ownership, version history, or any other mutable characteristic.

Every Canonical Object Identifier shall conform to the official CWC-CAR Object ID format.

The standard Object ID structure is:

```text
CWC-OBJ-000001
```

The format consists of the following components:

| Component | Description |
|-----------|-------------|
| **CWC** | Fixed ecosystem prefix identifying the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System. |
| **OBJ** | Fixed identifier indicating a Canonical Asset Object. |
| **000001** | Sequential numeric identifier assigned by CWC-CAR. |

Conceptually, the Object ID structure is represented as follows:

```text
CWC
 │
 ├── OBJ
 │      │
 │      └── 000001
```

The numeric sequence is assigned sequentially by the Canonical Asset Registry and shall never be reused, regardless of the lifecycle state of the corresponding Registry Record.

The Object ID Format is intentionally stable and semantically neutral. Classification, ownership, lifecycle state, specifications, and other descriptive information are maintained separately within the Registry Record and shall never be encoded into the Object ID itself.

By adopting a simple, permanent, and implementation-independent identifier format, CWC-CAR ensures long-term interoperability, scalability, and canonical consistency across the entire Coz We Care Canonical Asset System.

---

## E.2 Object ID Assignment

**Object ID Assignment** defines the canonical process by which a **Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID)** is assigned to a Canonical Asset Object within the **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)**.

The purpose of Object ID Assignment is to ensure that every Canonical Asset Object receives one—and only one—permanent identifier through a controlled, consistent, and authoritative registration process.

Object ID Assignment is performed exclusively by CWC-CAR. No other canonical project, registry, specification, or reference document is authorized to create, assign, modify, or reassign Canonical Object Identifiers.

Every Canonical Asset Object shall receive its Object ID only after successfully completing the official registration process.

The Object ID Assignment process follows the sequence below:

```text
Candidate Object
        │
        ▼
Registration Review
        │
        ▼
Registration Approval
        │
        ▼
Object ID Assignment
        │
        ▼
Registry Record Creation
        │
        ▼
Registered Canonical Asset Object
```

The Object ID Assignment process shall satisfy the following requirements:

- Every Canonical Asset Object shall receive exactly one Object ID.
- An Object ID shall be assigned only once.
- Object IDs shall be assigned sequentially according to the official registry sequence.
- Assigned Object IDs shall never be modified, recycled, or reassigned.
- Failed or withdrawn registration proposals shall not receive Object IDs.
- The assignment process shall preserve uniqueness, traceability, and long-term registry integrity.

Once an Object ID has been assigned, it becomes the permanent canonical identity of the registered object. Subsequent changes to the object's name, classification, lifecycle status, specification, governance, implementation, or reference representation shall never affect the assigned Object ID.

By centralizing Object ID Assignment within CWC-CAR, the Canonical Asset System ensures that every Canonical Asset Object possesses a single authoritative identity that remains stable, unique, and permanently traceable throughout its entire lifecycle.

---

## E.3 Object ID Uniqueness

**Object ID Uniqueness** establishes the canonical rules that guarantee every **Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID)** within the **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)** is globally unique.

The purpose of Object ID Uniqueness is to ensure that every Canonical Asset Object can be identified unambiguously throughout the Canonical Asset System. Uniqueness eliminates duplicate identities, prevents identifier collisions, and preserves the integrity of all canonical references.

Every Object ID shall uniquely identify one—and only one—Canonical Asset Object.

The uniqueness of an Object ID is defined by the following principles:

- An Object ID shall identify exactly one Canonical Asset Object.
- No two Registry Records shall share the same Object ID.
- An Object ID shall never be reassigned to another object.
- An Object ID shall never be duplicated.
- An Object ID shall remain unique regardless of the object's lifecycle state.
- Archived, Deprecated, or Replaced Registry Records shall permanently retain their assigned Object IDs.

Conceptually, Object ID uniqueness is represented as follows:

```text
Object ID A ─────► Canonical Asset Object A

Object ID B ─────► Canonical Asset Object B

Object ID C ─────► Canonical Asset Object C
```

The reverse relationship is also true:

```text
Canonical Asset Object A ─────► Object ID A

Canonical Asset Object B ─────► Object ID B

Canonical Asset Object C ─────► Object ID C
```

This establishes a permanent one-to-one correspondence between a Canonical Asset Object and its assigned Object ID.

Object ID Uniqueness shall be maintained throughout the entire lifecycle of the registry. Even if a Registry Record becomes Deprecated, Archived, or is superseded by another Canonical Asset Object, its assigned Object ID shall remain permanently reserved and shall never be reused.

By enforcing Object ID Uniqueness, CWC-CAR preserves the integrity, stability, traceability, and interoperability of the Canonical Asset System while ensuring that every Canonical Asset Object possesses one permanent and unambiguous canonical identity.

---

## E.4 Object ID Immutability

**Object ID Immutability** establishes the canonical principle that every **Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID)** assigned by the **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)** is permanent and shall never be altered throughout the lifecycle of the corresponding Canonical Asset Object.

The purpose of Object ID Immutability is to preserve the long-term stability of canonical identity across the entire Coz We Care Canonical Asset System. By ensuring that Object IDs remain unchanged, CWC-CAR guarantees that canonical references, architectural relationships, specifications, and historical records remain permanently valid.

Once assigned, an Object ID becomes the immutable identity of its associated Canonical Asset Object.

Object ID Immutability is governed by the following principles:

- An assigned Object ID shall never be modified.
- An Object ID shall never be transferred to another Canonical Asset Object.
- An Object ID shall never be regenerated due to changes in object name, classification, or specification.
- An Object ID shall remain unchanged regardless of lifecycle transitions, including Active, Deprecated, or Archived states.
- An Object ID shall remain valid even if the associated Canonical Asset Object is replaced, extended, or superseded.

Conceptually, Object ID Immutability is represented as follows:

```text
Object Registration
        │
        ▼
Object ID Assigned
        │
        ├────────────► Name Changes
        ├────────────► Classification Changes
        ├────────────► Specification Updates
        ├────────────► Lifecycle Changes
        ├────────────► Relationship Changes
        └────────────► Governance Changes

             │

             ▼

      Same Object ID
```

Immutability applies exclusively to the Canonical Object Identifier. Other elements of a Registry Record—including object name, description, classification, relationships, metadata, and lifecycle status—may evolve over time through approved registry maintenance processes.

The permanence of Object IDs is fundamental to the integrity of the Canonical Asset Registry. It enables stable cross-referencing, reliable historical traceability, and long-term interoperability across all canonical projects.

By enforcing Object ID Immutability, CWC-CAR ensures that every Canonical Asset Object possesses a single permanent identity that remains unchanged throughout its entire canonical existence.

---

## E.5 Object ID Reservation

**Object ID Reservation** defines the canonical rules governing the permanent reservation of **Canonical Object Identifiers (Object IDs)** within the **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)**.

The purpose of Object ID Reservation is to ensure that every assigned Object ID remains permanently reserved for its associated Canonical Asset Object. Reservation prevents identifier reuse, eliminates ambiguity, and preserves the historical integrity of the Canonical Asset System.

Once an Object ID has been assigned, it becomes permanently reserved, regardless of the subsequent lifecycle of the corresponding Registry Record.

Object ID Reservation is governed by the following principles:

- An assigned Object ID shall remain permanently reserved.
- Reserved Object IDs shall never be reused for another Canonical Asset Object.
- Deprecation, archival, replacement, or retirement shall not release a reserved Object ID.
- Reserved Object IDs shall remain traceable throughout the lifetime of the Canonical Asset Registry.
- Gaps in the numeric sequence resulting from withdrawn registrations or reserved allocations shall not be reassigned if the registry policy explicitly preserves them.

Conceptually, Object ID Reservation is represented as follows:

```text
Object ID Assigned
        │
        ▼
 Permanently Reserved
        │
        ├── Active
        ├── Deprecated
        ├── Archived
        └── Replaced

        │

        ▼

 Never Reused
```

Object ID Reservation applies only to Canonical Object Identifiers. It does not prevent Registry Records from evolving through updates to names, classifications, metadata, relationships, or lifecycle states. Those changes shall never affect the reservation status of the assigned Object ID.

By permanently reserving every assigned Object ID, CWC-CAR preserves the stability, uniqueness, historical continuity, and long-term integrity of the Canonical Asset Registry while ensuring that every Canonical Asset Object remains permanently identifiable.

---

## E.6 Object ID Retirement

**Object ID Retirement** defines the canonical rules governing the administrative retirement of **Canonical Object Identifiers (Object IDs)** within the **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)**.

The purpose of Object ID Retirement is to clarify that while a Registry Record may transition to an inactive administrative state, the associated Object ID itself is never retired, deleted, recycled, or reassigned. Retirement applies only to the operational use of a Registry Record and never to its canonical identity.

Within CWC-CAR, an Object ID is considered permanently active as an identifier, regardless of the administrative status of the associated Registry Record.

Object ID Retirement follows the following principles:

- An Object ID shall never be retired independently of its Registry Record.
- An Object ID shall never be deleted from the Canonical Asset Registry.
- Retirement of a Registry Record shall not invalidate its assigned Object ID.
- Archived and Deprecated Registry Records shall permanently retain their Object IDs.
- Object IDs remain valid historical references indefinitely.

Conceptually, Object ID Retirement is represented as follows:

```text
Object ID Assigned
        │
        ▼
 Registry Record
        │
        ├── Active
        ├── Deprecated
        └── Archived

        │

        ▼

 Object ID Remains Valid Forever
```

The administrative retirement of a Registry Record does not affect the permanence of the Canonical Object Identifier. Historical references, architectural relationships, canonical specifications, and reference representations shall continue to use the original Object ID without modification.

Consequently, CWC-CAR recognizes that **Registry Records may become inactive, but Canonical Object Identifiers never do**. The identity established by an Object ID is permanent and remains valid for the lifetime of the Canonical Asset Registry.

By maintaining permanent Object IDs regardless of registry lifecycle, CWC-CAR guarantees historical continuity, architectural stability, and long-term interoperability across the entire Coz We Care Canonical Asset System.

---

## E.7 Object ID Traceability

**Object ID Traceability** defines the canonical rules ensuring that every **Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID)** remains permanently traceable throughout the lifecycle of its corresponding **Canonical Asset Object** within the **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)**.

The purpose of Object ID Traceability is to preserve an uninterrupted historical record of every Canonical Asset Object, enabling consistent identification, navigation, governance, auditing, and interoperability across the entire Canonical Asset System.

Traceability ensures that an Object ID always refers to the same Canonical Asset Object regardless of changes to the Registry Record, object name, classification, specification, governance, or lifecycle status.

Object ID Traceability is governed by the following principles:

- Every Object ID shall remain permanently associated with exactly one Canonical Asset Object.
- Every Registry Record shall remain traceable through its assigned Object ID.
- Historical changes shall preserve Object ID continuity.
- Registry relationships shall reference Object IDs rather than mutable object attributes.
- Canonical references within CWC-CAB and CWC-CRS shall use the assigned Object ID as the primary identity reference.
- Traceability shall be maintained regardless of lifecycle transitions, including Active, Deprecated, and Archived states.

Conceptually, Object ID Traceability is represented as follows:

```text
Object ID
    │
    ├── Registry Record
    ├── Lifecycle History
    ├── Classification History
    ├── Relationship History
    ├── Canonical Specification
    └── Reference Representation
```

Traceability applies to the complete history of a Canonical Asset Object. Updates to metadata, specifications, governance documents, or reference representations shall extend the historical record without interrupting the continuity established by the Object ID.

The Object ID therefore functions as the permanent anchor connecting every canonical artifact associated with a Canonical Asset Object throughout its existence.

By maintaining comprehensive Object ID Traceability, CWC-CAR ensures complete historical continuity, reliable architectural navigation, consistent cross-referencing, and long-term governance across the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System.

---

## E.8 Object ID Governance

**Object ID Governance** defines the authority, responsibilities, and governance principles governing the creation, assignment, protection, and long-term management of **Canonical Object Identifiers (Object IDs)** within the **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)**.

The purpose of Object ID Governance is to ensure that every Canonical Object Identifier remains authoritative, unique, permanent, and consistently managed throughout the lifetime of the Canonical Asset System.

Object ID Governance establishes CWC-CAR as the sole authority responsible for the governance of Canonical Object Identifiers.

The governance of Object IDs is based on the following principles:

- Only CWC-CAR may create Canonical Object Identifiers.
- Every Canonical Asset Object shall receive exactly one Object ID.
- Object IDs shall never be modified after assignment.
- Object IDs shall never be reassigned to another Canonical Asset Object.
- Object IDs shall never be duplicated.
- Object IDs shall never be reused after assignment.
- All canonical projects shall recognize CWC-CAR as the authoritative source of Object IDs.
- Every canonical reference shall use the assigned Object ID as the authoritative identity of the corresponding Canonical Asset Object.

Conceptually, Object ID Governance is represented as follows:

```text
                CWC-CAR
                   │
                   ▼
      Object ID Governance Authority
                   │
        ┌──────────┼──────────┐
        ▼          ▼          ▼
 Assignment   Protection   Maintenance
        │          │          │
        └──────────┼──────────┘
                   ▼
          Canonical Object ID
```

Object ID Governance applies exclusively to the management of Canonical Object Identifiers. It does not govern object specifications, reference representations, implementation details, production assets, or lifecycle management beyond the preservation of canonical identity.

By establishing centralized governance for Canonical Object Identifiers, CWC-CAR ensures that every Canonical Asset Object possesses one authoritative identity that remains consistent, traceable, interoperable, and permanently protected throughout the evolution of the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System.

---

# Appendix F — Registry Record Template

The **Registry Record Template** defines the canonical structure used to document every **Canonical Asset Object** registered within the **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)**.

The purpose of the Registry Record Template is to provide a standardized, complete, and consistent representation of every Registry Record maintained by CWC-CAR. By using a single canonical template, the registry ensures that all Canonical Asset Objects are documented uniformly while preserving interoperability, traceability, and long-term maintainability.

The Registry Record Template is the operational implementation of the **Registry Schema** defined in **Appendix A**. Every Registry Record shall conform to this template.

The Registry Record Template records the identity, classification, relationships, lifecycle, references, and metadata of a Canonical Asset Object without describing its specification, implementation, or visual representation.

Conceptually, the Registry Record Template is represented as follows:

```text
Registry Record
       │
       ├── Object Identity
       ├── Object Classification
       ├── Object Relationships
       ├── Registry Lifecycle
       ├── Canonical References
       └── Registry Metadata
```

The following sections define the standard fields that constitute an official Registry Record within CWC-CAR.

---

## F.1 Registry Record Header

The **Registry Record Header** is the opening section of every Registry Record within the **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)**. It provides the essential identification information required to uniquely recognize, classify, and manage a registered Canonical Asset Object.

The purpose of the Registry Record Header is to establish the official identity of a Registry Record before presenting its classifications, relationships, lifecycle information, canonical references, and administrative metadata.

Every Registry Record shall contain exactly one Registry Record Header.

The Registry Record Header consists of the following mandatory fields:

| Field | Description |
|-------|-------------|
| **Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID)** | The permanent and unique identifier assigned by CWC-CAR. |
| **Object Name** | The official canonical name of the registered object. |
| **Object Type** | The primary architectural type of the object. |
| **Registry Status** | The current administrative status of the Registry Record. |
| **Current Lifecycle State** | The current lifecycle stage of the registered object. |
| **Current Version** | The current version of the Registry Record. |

A standard Registry Record Header is structured as follows:

```text
Object ID
Object Name
Object Type
Registry Status
Lifecycle State
Record Version
```

The Registry Record Header establishes only the canonical identity and administrative status of the registered object. It does not describe the object's specification, implementation, governance, production guidance, or visual representation.

Every subsequent section of the Registry Record shall reference the identity established by the Registry Record Header.

By standardizing the Registry Record Header, CWC-CAR ensures that every Registry Record begins with a consistent, authoritative, and immediately recognizable identity structure, supporting efficient navigation, traceability, and interoperability across the Canonical Asset System.

---

## F.2 Object Identity

The **Object Identity** section records the permanent identity of the registered **Canonical Asset Object**. It is the authoritative component of every Registry Record and establishes the object that is being registered within the **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)**.

The purpose of the Object Identity section is to ensure that every Registry Record contains sufficient information to uniquely identify its corresponding Canonical Asset Object independently of its specification, implementation, governance, or visual representation.

The information contained within this section represents the canonical identity of the object and shall remain permanently associated with its assigned Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID).

The Object Identity section consists of the following fields:

| Field | Description |
|-------|-------------|
| **Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID)** | The permanent identifier assigned by CWC-CAR. |
| **Object Name** | The official canonical name of the object. |
| **Alternative Name(s)** | Optional aliases or historical names recognized by the registry. |
| **Object Type** | The primary architectural type of the object. |
| **Object Summary** | A concise description of the object's conceptual identity. |
| **Identity Notes** | Optional notes supporting object identification. |

A standard Object Identity section is represented as follows:

```text
Object ID
Object Name
Alternative Name(s)
Object Type
Object Summary
Identity Notes
```

The Object Identity section defines only the permanent identity of the registered Canonical Asset Object. It does not include classification details, lifecycle information, relationships, canonical specifications, or administrative metadata.

The Canonical Object Identifier remains the primary identity reference for all information contained within the Registry Record.

By standardizing the Object Identity section, CWC-CAR ensures that every Canonical Asset Object possesses a consistent, stable, and authoritative identity that remains permanently recognizable throughout the entire Canonical Asset System.

---

## F.3 Object Classification

The **Object Classification** section records the official classification assigned to a registered **Canonical Asset Object** within the **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)**.

The purpose of the Object Classification section is to organize Canonical Asset Objects according to the official **Object Classification Taxonomy** defined by CWC-CAR. Classification provides architectural context for the registered object while remaining completely independent of its canonical identity.

The information contained within this section supports registry organization, navigation, discovery, and interoperability without affecting the Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID).

The Object Classification section consists of the following fields:

| Field | Description |
|-------|-------------|
| **Domain** | The highest-level architectural grouping of the object. |
| **Category** | The functional grouping within the assigned Domain. |
| **Type** | The primary architectural type of the object. |
| **Subtype** | Optional specialization of the object type. |
| **Classification Notes** | Optional explanatory notes supporting the assigned classification. |

A standard Object Classification section is represented as follows:

```text
Domain
Category
Type
Subtype
Classification Notes
```

Object Classification provides organizational context only. It shall never determine, modify, or replace the canonical identity established by the assigned Canonical Object Identifier.

Changes to classification may occur as the Canonical Asset System evolves. Such changes shall not affect the Registry Record, Object ID, lifecycle history, canonical references, or architectural relationships of the registered Canonical Asset Object.

By standardizing the Object Classification section, CWC-CAR ensures that every Registry Record is consistently organized according to a common architectural taxonomy, supporting scalable registry management and long-term semantic consistency across the Canonical Asset System.

---

## F.4 Object Relationships

The **Object Relationships** section records the official relationships between a registered **Canonical Asset Object** and other Canonical Asset Objects within the **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)**.

The purpose of the Object Relationships section is to provide a complete and traceable representation of the architectural connections maintained by the registered object. These relationships establish how the object interacts with other Canonical Asset Objects while preserving the independence and permanence of every Canonical Object Identifier.

Every relationship recorded in this section shall reference only registered Canonical Asset Objects through their assigned Canonical Object Identifiers (Object IDs).

The Object Relationships section consists of the following fields:

| Field | Description |
|-------|-------------|
| **Relationship Type** | The standardized Relationship Type defined by CWC-CAR. |
| **Related Object ID** | The Canonical Object Identifier of the related object. |
| **Relationship Direction** | Indicates the direction of the relationship, where applicable. |
| **Relationship Description** | Optional explanation of the relationship. |
| **Relationship Status** | Indicates whether the relationship is active or historical. |

A standard Object Relationships section is represented as follows:

```text
Relationship Type
Related Object ID
Relationship Direction
Relationship Description
Relationship Status
```

A Registry Record may contain zero, one, or multiple Object Relationships depending on the architectural context of the registered Canonical Asset Object.

Relationships recorded within this section shall use only the standardized Relationship Types defined in **Appendix C — Relationship Types**. The introduction of additional relationship instances shall not modify the identity, lifecycle, or governance of any participating Canonical Asset Object.

By standardizing the Object Relationships section, CWC-CAR provides a consistent mechanism for documenting architectural connections, supporting registry navigation, dependency analysis, semantic interoperability, and long-term traceability throughout the Canonical Asset System.

---

## F.5 Registry Lifecycle

The **Registry Lifecycle** section records the current lifecycle state and historical lifecycle progression of a registered **Canonical Asset Object** within the **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)**.

The purpose of the Registry Lifecycle section is to provide a standardized representation of the administrative progression of a Registry Record throughout its existence. Lifecycle information communicates the current standing of the registered object while preserving its permanent canonical identity.

The Registry Lifecycle section applies exclusively to the Registry Record maintained by CWC-CAR. It does not describe implementation maturity, production readiness, publication status, or operational usage.

The Registry Lifecycle section consists of the following fields:

| Field | Description |
|-------|-------------|
| **Current Lifecycle State** | The current lifecycle state of the Registry Record. |
| **Effective Date** | The date on which the current lifecycle state became effective. |
| **Previous Lifecycle State** | The immediately preceding lifecycle state, if applicable. |
| **Transition Reason** | The rationale for the lifecycle transition. |
| **Lifecycle History** | Historical record of previous lifecycle transitions. |
| **Lifecycle Notes** | Optional administrative notes regarding lifecycle management. |

A standard Registry Lifecycle section is represented as follows:

```text
Current Lifecycle State
Effective Date
Previous Lifecycle State
Transition Reason
Lifecycle History
Lifecycle Notes
```

Lifecycle information documents the administrative evolution of the Registry Record only. Changes to lifecycle states shall never modify the Canonical Object Identifier, object classification, architectural relationships, or canonical references associated with the registered Canonical Asset Object.

Lifecycle transitions shall follow the Registry Status model defined in **Appendix D — Registry Status**, ensuring consistent governance and long-term historical traceability across the Canonical Asset Registry.

By standardizing the Registry Lifecycle section, CWC-CAR provides a consistent mechanism for documenting the administrative evolution of Registry Records while preserving the permanence, stability, and integrity of every Canonical Asset Object.

---

## F.6 Canonical References

The **Canonical References** section records the official references linking a registered **Canonical Asset Object** to its corresponding canonical documents within the **Coz We Care Canonical Asset System**.

The purpose of the Canonical References section is to establish traceable connections between the Registry Record maintained by **CWC-CAR** and the canonical artifacts maintained by related canonical projects. These references ensure interoperability while preserving the separation of responsibilities between identity, specification, and reference representation.

Canonical References shall always originate from the Canonical Object Identifier assigned by CWC-CAR. The Registry Record serves as the authoritative identity source from which all canonical references are derived.

The Canonical References section consists of the following fields:

| Field | Description |
|-------|-------------|
| **Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID)** | The permanent identifier serving as the reference anchor. |
| **CWC-CAB Reference** | Reference to the Canonical Asset Specification maintained by CWC-CAB. |
| **CWC-CRS Reference** | Reference to the Canonical Reference Sheet maintained by CWC-CRS. |
| **Related Registry Records** | References to other associated Registry Records within CWC-CAR. |
| **External Canonical References** | Optional references to approved external canonical resources. |
| **Reference Notes** | Optional notes supporting canonical traceability. |

A standard Canonical References section is represented as follows:

```text
Canonical Object Identifier
CWC-CAB Reference
CWC-CRS Reference
Related Registry Records
External Canonical References
Reference Notes
```

Canonical References establish navigational and traceability links only. They shall never define, modify, replace, or duplicate the canonical identity established by the assigned Canonical Object Identifier.

All canonical projects within the Coz We Care Canonical Asset System shall reference Canonical Asset Objects through the Object ID maintained by CWC-CAR. This ensures that every specification, reference representation, and architectural relationship remains consistently anchored to a single authoritative identity.

By standardizing the Canonical References section, CWC-CAR provides a reliable mechanism for connecting Registry Records with their associated canonical artifacts while preserving the stability, interoperability, and long-term consistency of the Canonical Asset System.

---

## F.7 Registry Metadata

The **Registry Metadata** section records the administrative information associated with a **Registry Record** within the **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)**.

The purpose of the Registry Metadata section is to support the governance, administration, maintenance, and historical traceability of Registry Records without affecting the canonical identity of the registered Canonical Asset Object.

Registry Metadata describes the Registry Record itself rather than the Canonical Asset Object it represents. Consequently, changes to Registry Metadata shall never modify the assigned Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID), object classification, architectural relationships, or canonical references.

The Registry Metadata section consists of the following fields:

| Field | Description |
|-------|-------------|
| **Registry Record Version** | The current version of the Registry Record. |
| **Registry Status** | The current administrative status of the Registry Record. |
| **Registration Date** | The official date on which the Canonical Asset Object was registered. |
| **Last Updated** | The date of the most recent modification to the Registry Record. |
| **Registry Authority** | The authority responsible for maintaining the Registry Record. |
| **Change History** | References to significant administrative modifications affecting the Registry Record. |
| **Administrative Notes** | Optional notes supporting registry administration and maintenance. |

A standard Registry Metadata section is represented as follows:

```text
Registry Record Version
Registry Status
Registration Date
Last Updated
Registry Authority
Change History
Administrative Notes
```

Registry Metadata provides operational context for registry administration while remaining completely independent of the canonical identity of the registered object. Administrative updates, editorial revisions, and governance activities shall affect only the Registry Record and shall never alter the assigned Canonical Object Identifier.

Registry Metadata enables long-term registry governance by preserving a complete administrative history of every Registry Record, supporting auditing, maintenance, and historical traceability throughout the Canonical Asset System.

By standardizing the Registry Metadata section, CWC-CAR ensures that every Registry Record contains consistent administrative information while preserving the permanence, stability, and integrity of every registered Canonical Asset Object.

---

## F.8 Registry Record Example

This section provides a standardized example of a **Registry Record** within the **Coz We Care Canonical Asset Registry (CWC-CAR)**. The example demonstrates how the Registry Record Template defined in this appendix may be applied to a registered Canonical Asset Object.

The purpose of this example is to illustrate the structure of a Registry Record rather than to define the specification, implementation, or visual representation of the registered object.

The values shown below are illustrative only.

### Example Registry Record

| Field | Value |
|-------|-------|
| **Canonical Object Identifier (Object ID)** | CWC-OBJ-000001 |
| **Object Name** | Official Logo |
| **Alternative Name(s)** | — |
| **Object Type** | Brand Asset |
| **Object Summary** | The official logo of the Coz We Care ecosystem. |
| **Domain** | Brand |
| **Category** | Visual Identity |
| **Type** | Logo |
| **Subtype** | Primary Logo |
| **Registry Status** | Active |
| **Lifecycle State** | Active |
| **Relationship(s)** | Referenced By: CWC-CAB, CWC-CRS |
| **CWC-CAB Reference** | Canonical Asset Specification |
| **CWC-CRS Reference** | Canonical Reference Sheet |
| **Registration Date** | YYYY-MM-DD |
| **Last Updated** | YYYY-MM-DD |
| **Registry Record Version** | 1.0 |
| **Registry Authority** | CWC-CAR |

The same Registry Record may also be represented using the canonical Registry Record layout:

```text
====================================================
Canonical Object Identifier : CWC-OBJ-000001

Object Name                 : Official Logo
Alternative Name(s)         : -
Object Type                 : Brand Asset
Object Summary              : Official Coz We Care logo

--------------------------------------------

Classification

Domain                      : Brand
Category                    : Visual Identity
Type                        : Logo
Subtype                     : Primary Logo

--------------------------------------------

Relationships

Referenced By               : CWC-CAB
Referenced By               : CWC-CRS

--------------------------------------------

Lifecycle

Registry Status             : Active
Lifecycle State             : Active

--------------------------------------------

Canonical References

CWC-CAB                     : Canonical Specification
CWC-CRS                     : Canonical Reference Sheet

--------------------------------------------

Registry Metadata

Record Version              : 1.0
Registration Date           : YYYY-MM-DD
Last Updated                : YYYY-MM-DD
Registry Authority          : CWC-CAR
====================================================
```

The Registry Record illustrated above is intended solely as a canonical template. Individual Registry Records may contain additional values where permitted by the Registry Schema, provided that the standardized structure defined by CWC-CAR is preserved.

By maintaining a single standardized Registry Record format, CWC-CAR ensures that every Canonical Asset Object is registered in a uniform, consistent, and machine-readable manner while supporting long-term governance, interoperability, and traceability across the entire Coz We Care Canonical Asset System.

---

